And I'm really excited to dive into it with you.

49
00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:21.060
Before we do that though,

50
00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:23.430
can we geek out on Michigan for a minute?

51
00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:26.020
We have to, we have multiple things to geek out on.

52
00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:28.620
Let me just say thank you, your story obviously

53
00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:30.500
has been a huge inspiration to so many people.

54
00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:32.950
But every time I look at what you've accomplished

55
00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:37.230
as an athlete, I just think, I don't know how you function

56
00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:39.870
in the world, I would love to have your endurance.

57
00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:42.520
Well, that sensibility is mutual,

58
00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:45.560
because I'm fascinated with how you function in the world.

59
00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:47.820
I don't know how you get so much stuff done

60
00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:49.820
and how you excel in so many areas.

61
00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:53.530
But, perhaps we can get into it a little bit.

62
00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:56.850
I mean, we have, you know, I'm much older than you but,

63
00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:58.840
like yourself, I grew up in Michigan,

64
00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:02.123
we moved to Washington DC when I was like seven or eight.

65
00:03:03.351 --> 00:03:08.080
But I'm from Grosse Pointe, my dad was a Detroit lawyer.

66
00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:12.980
My mom was an educator, I was a swimmer, you were a diver,

67
00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:16.483
everybody in my family bleeds Maize And Blue,

68
00:03:17.508 --> 00:03:20.730
and it's just cool to talk to somebody

69
00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:23.423
who's somewhat of the same world.

70
00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:26.970
And I think we might even know

71
00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:29.430
some of the same people from that world.

72
00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:30.580
Like when you went to Harvard,

73
00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:33.420
was Joe Brunel still the swim coach there?

74
00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:36.425
No, I think it was Tim Murphy when I got there.

75
00:03:36.425 --> 00:03:38.690
That was, okay, so a little bit later.

76
00:03:38.690 --> 00:03:42.660
I vividly recall I had a kind of an inflection point

77
00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:45.730
in my life when I went to University of Michigan

78
00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:47.950
on a recruiting trip for the swim team

79
00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:51.830
and had some interesting moments with Bruce Kimball

80
00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:54.440
and met Dick Kimball and you know,

81
00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:56.820
I'm sort of connected to that community.

82
00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:59.690
My grandfather was captain of the University

83
00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:02.710
of Michigan swim team like back in like 1929,

84
00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:05.463
under Matt Mann who the pool is named after.

85
00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:10.170
Amazing, I didn't realize your roots were this deep, okay.

86
00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:13.490
Yeah, my dad went to law school at Michigan,

87
00:04:13.490 --> 00:04:17.950
my mom went to University of Michigan so, lots of overlap.

88
00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:19.270
Yeah, there's a ton of overlap here.

89
00:04:19.270 --> 00:04:23.410
So we share mom's a teacher, dad's a lawyer,

90
00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:28.070
Detroit suburbs, I found out after I became a diver

91
00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:30.260
that my grandmother had been a diver.

92
00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:32.080
She never had mentioned it to me,

93
00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:34.343
and it just came out one day.

94
00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:39.020
My mom went to Michigan, my uncle is the world's

95
00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:41.610
most rabid Michigan fan and went to law school there,

96
00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:45.390
and I think he hasn't missed an in person game in 50 years.

97
00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:48.160
And I think not going to Michigan

98
00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:50.656
for college probably broke my mom's heart.

99
00:04:50.656 --> 00:04:52.190
(Rich laughs)

100
00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:53.023
What are you doing?

101
00:04:53.023 --> 00:04:54.720
It's like leaving a religion?

102
00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:57.240
I know, especially there's something unique

103
00:04:57.240 --> 00:04:59.420
about Michigan in that regard too.

104
00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:02.880
It deftly was similar in my case as well.

105
00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:04.670
You felt that too and then wow,

106
00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:06.540
so that's also diving lower.

107
00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:10.630
I mean, the the Kimball family, obviously they,

108
00:05:10.630 --> 00:05:12.830
I guess they belong to a place of their own.

109
00:05:12.830 --> 00:05:15.590
I remember, I actually thought about not applying

110
00:05:15.590 --> 00:05:18.480
to Michigan for undergrad, because I was so terrified

111
00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:21.710
of the thought of having Dick Kimball as a diving coach.

112
00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:24.270
I remember, you know, friends who insisted

113
00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:26.450
on only doing one in three meters springboard,

114
00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:28.770
being forced to become 10 meter platform divers,

115
00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:31.230
and then coughing up blood when they crash.

116
00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:32.290
I didn't wanna be that guy.

117
00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:34.620
Yeah, I mean, it's legendary.

118
00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:37.830
And I've told this story before, but the experience

119
00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:40.800
that I had on this recruiting trip, I was at a party,

120
00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:44.520
a house party after swimming and diving me.

121
00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:48.080
And Bruce Kimball handed me a beer,

122
00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:50.780
and then he proceeded to perform the greatest party trick

123
00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:53.960
that I'd ever seen, where he did a standing back flip,

124
00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:57.150
holding a cup of beer without spilling any of it,

125
00:05:57.150 --> 00:05:59.662
and I just thought that was the most amazing thing

126
00:05:59.662 --> 00:06:04.650
I ever had seen, then I went on my own journey

127
00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:08.020
with alcohol and alcoholism and recovery,

128
00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:11.070
and of course, the story of Bruce's is pretty well told

129
00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:14.600
and the kind of tragic circumstances that befell him.

130
00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:16.760
So sad.
Yeah.

131
00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:19.130
Did you dive at Harvard?

132
00:06:19.130 --> 00:06:21.090
I did, and you swam at Stanford, right?

133
00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:24.110
I did, yeah, did you dive all four years

134
00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:25.770
or just a year or two?

135
00:06:25.770 --> 00:06:29.010
I stopped after my first year, how'd you guess?

136
00:06:29.010 --> 00:06:31.210
I don't know, you got too busy with other things

137
00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:33.410
that seem to compel your attention.

138
00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:34.670
That's exactly what happened.

139
00:06:34.670 --> 00:06:37.790
Yeah, well, the new book is amazing,

140
00:06:37.790 --> 00:06:40.210
I can't think of a better book,

141
00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:43.570
more suited for this particular moment

142
00:06:43.570 --> 00:06:46.680
than the subjects that you're talking about right now.

143
00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:49.550
And in thinking about the book,

144
00:06:49.550 --> 00:06:51.730
and kind of culture at large, you know,

145
00:06:51.730 --> 00:06:54.610
I've spent a lot of time just thinking about

146
00:06:54.610 --> 00:06:58.910
how we communicate right now.

147
00:06:58.910 --> 00:07:03.910
And my sense is that we are so increasingly calcified

148
00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:10.340
in our belief systems and the identities

149
00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:14.700
that we form around them, and this is further intensified

150
00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:17.140
by social media algorithms

151
00:07:17.140 --> 00:07:19.880
that serve up what we wanna hear that,

152
00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:22.960
enhances our confirmation bias,

153
00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:25.620
the kind of tribes that we form online

154
00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:28.923
and in our analog lives that we're kind of,

155
00:07:30.290 --> 00:07:34.590
queuing our membership in good standing too.

156
00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:37.350
And there's, in my sense,

157
00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:39.980
there seems to be a velocity behind that,

158
00:07:39.980 --> 00:07:42.930
like it's becoming more and more intense,

159
00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:45.150
and its environmental, right?

160
00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:47.430
The idea being that this is just kinda happening

161
00:07:47.430 --> 00:07:51.230
to us without any willful input on our part.

162
00:07:51.230 --> 00:07:54.730
And the antidote, which is the thesis of your book

163
00:07:54.730 --> 00:07:58.420
requires a great deal of thought and presence

164
00:07:58.420 --> 00:08:00.750
of mind and intentionality.

165
00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:04.663
And I see it almost like a war between these two worlds.

166
00:08:06.354 --> 00:08:10.160
And it's difficult to be sanguine about the future,

167
00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:12.700
given the amount of intentionality

168
00:08:12.700 --> 00:08:14.710
that's required to kind of rebut

169
00:08:16.690 --> 00:08:18.580
the forces that be out there

170
00:08:18.580 --> 00:08:21.869
that are marshaling us in the opposite direction?

171
00:08:21.869 --> 00:08:25.110
I think that's true, I think it's part of the reason

172
00:08:25.110 --> 00:08:28.470
that I wanted to write "Think Again," is to try to unpack,

173
00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:31.390
is there anything we can do to get more open minded

174
00:08:31.390 --> 00:08:34.580
and to encourage other people to be that way too?

175
00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:37.200
I think I came out more hopeful than I started.

176
00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:39.310
I think for a while this book was that project

177
00:08:39.310 --> 00:08:41.360
that I hope someone else would pursue,

178
00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:43.150
someone else go figure this out?

179
00:08:43.150 --> 00:08:45.980
Get people to choose mental flexibility

180
00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:48.080
over foolish consistency.

181
00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:50.860
Get people to be humble and curious,

182
00:08:50.860 --> 00:08:55.850
as opposed to arrogant and adjust, nobody wrote the book.

183
00:08:55.850 --> 00:08:59.880
And eventually, I said, "All right, I need to take this on."

184
00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:03.240
Obviously you've been working on this book for a while.

185
00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:07.737
And it is current in the sense that it tackles COVID

186
00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:13.920
in some regard, but obviously predates the capital,

187
00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:18.760
insurrection, and aspects of QAnon and some of the other,

188
00:09:18.760 --> 00:09:21.110
kind of things that we're seeing unfold right now,

189
00:09:21.110 --> 00:09:23.970
when you were watching the capital insurrection,

190
00:09:23.970 --> 00:09:26.780
how are you thinking about the ideas presented

191
00:09:26.780 --> 00:09:31.730
in the book and how these these things kind of coalesce?

192
00:09:31.730 --> 00:09:35.210
It was a shock, was the first thing, I never thought,

193
00:09:35.210 --> 00:09:37.790
that an insurrection would happen in America.

194
00:09:37.790 --> 00:09:40.280
It's something you read about happening in other countries,

195
00:09:40.280 --> 00:09:42.800
and thank your lucky stars that we don't live

196
00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:44.650
in one of those countries.

197
00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:48.360
And you know, as an organizational psychologist,

198
00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:49.870
I've spent most of my careers trying

199
00:09:49.870 --> 00:09:51.970
to study things that are timeless,

200
00:09:51.970 --> 00:09:54.940
and I've really deliberately resisted the timely,

201
00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:57.640
because I'm interested in ideas, not news.

202
00:09:57.640 --> 00:09:59.820
I'm interested in you know, in rigorous evidence,

203
00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:04.820
not current events, and yet, this idea of thinking again,

204
00:10:05.257 --> 00:10:08.160
I finished the book right around a time when we're dealing

205
00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:10.420
with the greatest polarization I've ever seen,

206
00:10:10.420 --> 00:10:13.720
and then an attack on our very democracy.

207
00:10:13.720 --> 00:10:15.313
And my first thought was,

208
00:10:16.730 --> 00:10:19.360
what everyone's now calling the big lie that,

209
00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:21.533
the election was stolen from Donald Trump.

210
00:10:22.370 --> 00:10:25.340
If we could have gotten many people to rethink

211
00:10:25.340 --> 00:10:27.540
their belief in that lie, it's possible

212
00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:29.890
the insurrection wouldn't have happened at all.

213
00:10:30.790 --> 00:10:33.530
And my next thought was, okay,

214
00:10:33.530 --> 00:10:35.680
most of these people don't seem to think

215
00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:37.610
that they're undermining democracy.

216
00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:40.090
They think they're saving democracy.

217
00:10:40.090 --> 00:10:43.550
And that requires a whole reexamination,

218
00:10:43.550 --> 00:10:47.540
of not just a belief, but a belief system, a worldview.

219
00:10:47.540 --> 00:10:51.620
And I don't I know that I know how to tackle that.

220
00:10:51.620 --> 00:10:54.910
I think I have some ideas and insights about how to begin

221
00:10:54.910 --> 00:10:57.360
to chip away at some of those wrong beliefs but,

222
00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:00.500
I think it's an extreme, I guess my reaction was,

223
00:11:00.500 --> 00:11:02.650
there's a sequel to this book that needs to be written

224
00:11:02.650 --> 00:11:06.690
about conspiracy theories, about false news,

225
00:11:06.690 --> 00:11:09.060
that is very different from how I was trying

226
00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:12.190
to deal with what I thought was everyday resistance

227
00:11:12.190 --> 00:11:13.850
to admitting that you're wrong,

228
00:11:13.850 --> 00:11:15.310
and to knowing what you don't know.

229
00:11:15.310 --> 00:11:18.670
And I think what your question makes me wonder is,

230
00:11:18.670 --> 00:11:21.920
is the everyday version of that now extreme enough

231
00:11:21.920 --> 00:11:24.800
that we need that book for more people than I realized.

232
00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:27.190
Yeah, I would like to read that book,

233
00:11:27.190 --> 00:11:30.660
I think there is something qualitatively different

234
00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:35.660
now about the way that we are magnetized by certain ideas.

235
00:11:37.800 --> 00:11:42.120
And the conspiracy theory is its own kind of genius

236
00:11:42.120 --> 00:11:44.810
in all of this, I mean, you did talk about it in the book,

237
00:11:44.810 --> 00:11:46.740
you have the example of the flat earther,

238
00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:49.690
and you have that graphic, that's circular graphic.

239
00:11:49.690 --> 00:11:54.690
But when you think about conspiracy theories, in general,

240
00:11:54.950 --> 00:11:59.220
you're dealing with a set of people who are under the belief

241
00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:02.220
that they're the ones who are thinking more open minded

242
00:12:02.220 --> 00:12:03.300
about this idea.

243
00:12:03.300 --> 00:12:05.380
They're the ones who have taken the red pill,

244
00:12:05.380 --> 00:12:09.110
they're seeing things that are, seeing things more clearly,

245
00:12:09.110 --> 00:12:12.240
they're standing outside of the conventional wisdom.

246
00:12:12.240 --> 00:12:14.600
And that makes it very different than some other kind

247
00:12:14.600 --> 00:12:18.840
of well held belief that informs identity.

248
00:12:18.840 --> 00:12:20.363
It does and it is.

249
00:12:22.180 --> 00:12:24.230
I think this is, it's a huge uphill battle.

250
00:12:24.230 --> 00:12:26.080
I've been reading a lot lately about the psychology

251
00:12:26.080 --> 00:12:28.130
of conspiracy theories, why people adopt them,

252
00:12:28.130 --> 00:12:30.630
and especially why or when

253
00:12:30.630 --> 00:12:32.490
they might be willing to reconsider them.

254
00:12:32.490 --> 00:12:35.140
And I think it's hard to have this conversation

255
00:12:35.140 --> 00:12:37.890
without talking about cognitive dissonance, right?

256
00:12:37.890 --> 00:12:41.200
Dating back to the classic Leon Festinger work,

257
00:12:41.200 --> 00:12:44.030
which basically said, "If you've already committed

258
00:12:44.030 --> 00:12:47.930
to a belief, then any threats to that belief,

259
00:12:47.930 --> 00:12:51.080
if they force you to treat the world

260
00:12:51.080 --> 00:12:56.047
as a less pleasant place for you to challenge your ego,

261
00:12:56.950 --> 00:13:00.280
to put aside, you know, pre existing commitments you've made

262
00:13:00.280 --> 00:13:03.320
to walk back statements you made in public,

263
00:13:03.320 --> 00:13:06.230
that it's easier just to assimilate whatever's going on

264
00:13:06.230 --> 00:13:08.760
into the statements you've already made,

265
00:13:08.760 --> 00:13:10.930
and defend your long standing beliefs,

266
00:13:10.930 --> 00:13:13.500
because then you avoid the discomfort of doubt,

267
00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:15.450
and you get the comfort of conviction."

268
00:13:16.530 --> 00:13:18.880
And I think that part is clear, right?

269
00:13:18.880 --> 00:13:20.440
We understand why that happens.

270
00:13:20.440 --> 00:13:22.400
I think the the undoing, the rethinking,

271
00:13:22.400 --> 00:13:26.440
the unlearning of those beliefs is a heck of a lot harder.

272
00:13:26.440 --> 00:13:28.470
And I think the most interesting thing

273
00:13:28.470 --> 00:13:31.770
that I've learned lately is that there's some research

274
00:13:31.770 --> 00:13:34.380
on what's called the cynical genius illusion,

275
00:13:34.380 --> 00:13:35.970
where when somebody is cynical,

276
00:13:35.970 --> 00:13:38.480
people tend to assume that means that they're smart,

277
00:13:38.480 --> 00:13:41.750
because they don't accept things at face value.

278
00:13:41.750 --> 00:13:43.100
It's actually the opposite.

279
00:13:43.960 --> 00:13:46.290
People who are extremely cynical tend to be lower

280
00:13:46.290 --> 00:13:48.510
in intelligence than their peers.

281
00:13:48.510 --> 00:13:50.130
And, of course, there's a big difference

282
00:13:50.130 --> 00:13:51.960
between cynicism and skepticism, right?

283
00:13:51.960 --> 00:13:55.140
Cynicism is not believing anything you hear.

284
00:13:55.140 --> 00:13:58.307
And skepticism is being a critical thinker and saying,

285
00:13:58.307 --> 00:14:00.406
"Show me the evidence."

286
00:14:00.406 --> 00:14:04.860
And one of the reasons that cynicism seems to emerge,

287
00:14:04.860 --> 00:14:08.950
is it's a defense mechanism against being manipulated.

288
00:14:08.950 --> 00:14:11.850
It's a defense mechanism that people gravitate toward,

289
00:14:11.850 --> 00:14:13.540
if they get manipulated a lot,

290
00:14:13.540 --> 00:14:15.363
because they're not that smart.

291
00:14:16.710 --> 00:14:20.980
And I think the ways that we try to attack that are just,

292
00:14:20.980 --> 00:14:23.700
it's like dumping fuel on a fire.

293
00:14:23.700 --> 00:14:26.270
Because the first thing that you know that quote, unquote,

294
00:14:26.270 --> 00:14:28.560
the elite does, or that the mainstream media does,

295
00:14:28.560 --> 00:14:31.860
or that universities do, is we go and accuse people

296
00:14:31.860 --> 00:14:34.130
of believing stupid things.

297
00:14:34.130 --> 00:14:36.570
And their fear is that they're not smart,

298
00:14:36.570 --> 00:14:37.470
and that's why they're clinging

299
00:14:37.470 --> 00:14:39.190
to these conspiracies in the first place,

300
00:14:39.190 --> 00:14:41.070
because they make them feel smart.

301
00:14:41.070 --> 00:14:43.750
And so I think we need to be really careful about you know,

302
00:14:43.750 --> 00:14:45.790
about threatening people's intelligence,

303
00:14:45.790 --> 00:14:50.017
about questioning people's ability

304
00:14:50.017 --> 00:14:53.288
to form judgments and reason.

305
00:14:53.288 --> 00:14:54.820
And I think there are better ways to surface that,

306
00:14:54.820 --> 00:14:55.690
which we could talk about,

307
00:14:55.690 --> 00:14:57.940
but that was an aha moment for me.

308
00:14:57.940 --> 00:15:02.129
Yeah, it seems like although intelligence,

309
00:15:02.129 --> 00:15:07.129
IQ and EQ are important, we also have to consider,

310
00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:11.150
other aspects of people's lives, like the extent

311
00:15:11.150 --> 00:15:14.720
to which they're disenfranchised, they're alienated,

312
00:15:14.720 --> 00:15:17.923
they're frustrated in their path and how those things,

313
00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:22.470
motivate people to cottoned on to ideas

314
00:15:22.470 --> 00:15:25.980
that otherwise they would think better of.

315
00:15:25.980 --> 00:15:27.500
Yeah, I think that's right.

316
00:15:27.500 --> 00:15:29.780
And one of the things I've learned,

317
00:15:29.780 --> 00:15:31.070
and you know this from the book,

318
00:15:31.070 --> 00:15:33.550
but I haven't, I certainly didn't think much

319
00:15:33.550 --> 00:15:36.670
about how to apply it to these kinds of conspiracy theories

320
00:15:36.670 --> 00:15:38.230
when I was writing the book.

321
00:15:38.230 --> 00:15:39.580
It turns out that how questions

322
00:15:39.580 --> 00:15:42.320
tend to be more powerful than why questions,

323
00:15:42.320 --> 00:15:43.910
you go to somebody who believes something

324
00:15:43.910 --> 00:15:47.310
that you think is is not consistent with the truth,

325
00:15:47.310 --> 00:15:49.400
and you ask them why they believe what they believe,

326
00:15:49.400 --> 00:15:51.500
and they have a bunch of pre made reasons

327
00:15:51.500 --> 00:15:53.480
that they find compelling.

328
00:15:53.480 --> 00:15:57.150
If instead, you ask them, "Well, how did that come to be?

329
00:15:57.150 --> 00:15:59.110
Or how does that work?"

330
00:15:59.110 --> 00:16:00.980
They're much more likely to see the gaps

331
00:16:00.980 --> 00:16:02.820
in their own understanding and to recognize

332
00:16:02.820 --> 00:16:04.190
the complexity of the issue.

333
00:16:04.190 --> 00:16:07.250
And I actually had a chance to apply this recently.

334
00:16:07.250 --> 00:16:10.230
I was talking with a friend who believes

335
00:16:10.230 --> 00:16:12.920
in some vaccine conspiracies,

336
00:16:12.920 --> 00:16:15.850
that the government and Big Pharma are in cahoots

337
00:16:15.850 --> 00:16:19.110
to basically pull the wool over your eyes and convince you

338
00:16:19.110 --> 00:16:22.720
that vaccines are much safer than they really are.

339
00:16:22.720 --> 00:16:27.160
I said, "Look, first of all, some conspiracies are real,

340
00:16:27.160 --> 00:16:28.910
let's put that on the table,

341
00:16:28.910 --> 00:16:30.820
and we can make a list of them.

342
00:16:30.820 --> 00:16:34.010
Secondly, I know that there are plenty of vaccines

343
00:16:34.010 --> 00:16:36.910
that have documented side effects and risks.

344
00:16:36.910 --> 00:16:39.420
And we can look even at the published evidence,

345
00:16:39.420 --> 00:16:40.827
right in peer reviewed journals and say,

346
00:16:40.827 --> 00:16:43.180
'Look, this is a complex issue.'

347
00:16:43.180 --> 00:16:44.810
What I wanna understand though,

348
00:16:44.810 --> 00:16:48.180
is that if this is so much worse than you think it is,

349
00:16:48.180 --> 00:16:51.410
why is there not a single scientist within here,

350
00:16:51.410 --> 00:16:54.200
who's willing to go against the establishment, right?

351
00:16:54.200 --> 00:16:57.150
In a world where the incentive is to be right,

352
00:16:57.150 --> 00:16:59.730
not to just toe the party line?

353
00:16:59.730 --> 00:17:03.400
And also, why can you not find a single credible journalist,

354
00:17:03.400 --> 00:17:05.520
who wants to win a Pulitzer Prize

355
00:17:05.520 --> 00:17:07.747
for exposing this conspiracy?"

356
00:17:08.780 --> 00:17:11.400
And he couldn't really answer those questions.

357
00:17:11.400 --> 00:17:12.760
And then I went a step further.

358
00:17:12.760 --> 00:17:15.010
And I said, "Okay, and just helped me think

359
00:17:15.010 --> 00:17:17.640
through how a giant government bureaucracy,

360
00:17:17.640 --> 00:17:19.390
and all these pharma companies,

361
00:17:19.390 --> 00:17:21.930
wouldn't have a single internal whistleblower?

362
00:17:21.930 --> 00:17:23.960
And how they, you think they're incompetent, right?

363
00:17:23.960 --> 00:17:25.974
You told me they're incompetent,

364
00:17:25.974 --> 00:17:29.070
how would they have even orchestrated this

365
00:17:29.070 --> 00:17:34.070
to keep the facts from everybody who's in that world?"

366
00:17:34.860 --> 00:17:36.847
And as he thought through how to do this, he said,

367
00:17:36.847 --> 00:17:41.847
"Well, you know, maybe they couldn't have maybe, you know,

368
00:17:41.900 --> 00:17:45.350
people just haven't wanted to publish the studies that,

369
00:17:45.350 --> 00:17:48.140
they cast doubt on vaccine efficacy or safety,

370
00:17:48.140 --> 00:17:50.490
and maybe those are under represented

371
00:17:50.490 --> 00:17:51.770
in the peer reviewed journals."

372
00:17:51.770 --> 00:17:55.270
And that was a much more reasonable place that he started.

373
00:17:55.270 --> 00:17:57.400
Right, so the idea and this was the subject

374
00:17:57.400 --> 00:18:00.320
of this new york times opinion piece, basically,

375
00:18:00.320 --> 00:18:02.960
that came out the other day,

376
00:18:02.960 --> 00:18:06.460
the idea being that you approach these conversations,

377
00:18:06.460 --> 00:18:10.040
rather than from the paradigm that you outlined in the book,

378
00:18:10.040 --> 00:18:14.260
which is, as prosecutor as preacher, or as politician,

379
00:18:14.260 --> 00:18:18.260
but rather lead with curiosity, ask a lot of questions,

380
00:18:18.260 --> 00:18:22.850
and receive the answers to those questions without judgment,

381
00:18:22.850 --> 00:18:26.340
but rather, more curiosity.

382
00:18:26.340 --> 00:18:28.310
That's the goal.
Is that accurate?

383
00:18:28.310 --> 00:18:31.890
Yeah, I think that's a great summary of both the mindset

384
00:18:31.890 --> 00:18:34.217
of approaching this like a scientist, and saying,

385
00:18:34.217 --> 00:18:35.770
"Look, I'm not gonna preach that I'm right,

386
00:18:35.770 --> 00:18:38.120
I'm not gonna prosecute you for being wrong.

387
00:18:38.120 --> 00:18:40.070
But I'm also not gonna be a politician,

388
00:18:40.070 --> 00:18:42.470
and just tell you what you wanna hear,

389
00:18:42.470 --> 00:18:44.820
to try to campaign for your approval."

390
00:18:44.820 --> 00:18:48.290
What I'm gonna do is, I'm gonna as a scientist,

391
00:18:48.290 --> 00:18:52.560
I'm gonna say, "Rich, this is totally fascinating to me,

392
00:18:52.560 --> 00:18:54.860
that your beliefs are so different from mine.

393
00:18:54.860 --> 00:18:57.360
And I genuinely wanna understand them better.

394
00:18:57.360 --> 00:19:00.890
And if you could help me make sense of how this works,

395
00:19:00.890 --> 00:19:02.450
and how this played out.

396
00:19:02.450 --> 00:19:05.590
And then, I also wanna understand,

397
00:19:05.590 --> 00:19:08.418
I'm sure I have some beliefs that are wrong,

398
00:19:08.418 --> 00:19:10.020
and I suspect you also do too, right?

399
00:19:10.020 --> 00:19:13.450
Nobody, omniscient, I'd love to understand

400
00:19:13.450 --> 00:19:15.580
what kind of information would lead you

401
00:19:15.580 --> 00:19:16.910
to consider changing your mind?

402
00:19:16.910 --> 00:19:18.740
And I'm also thrilled to talk

403
00:19:18.740 --> 00:19:20.240
about what might change my mind.

404
00:19:20.240 --> 00:19:22.150
And then the hope is, we're both gonna find out

405
00:19:22.150 --> 00:19:23.490
that our knowledge was incomplete

406
00:19:23.490 --> 00:19:24.440
through this conversation."

407
00:19:24.440 --> 00:19:26.983
And, of course, it doesn't work if that's a tactic, right?

408
00:19:26.983 --> 00:19:29.460
Because the other person will see right through it.

409
00:19:29.460 --> 00:19:31.570
But if you adopt what counseling psychologists

410
00:19:31.570 --> 00:19:35.080
called motivational interviewing, and you really try

411
00:19:35.080 --> 00:19:38.120
to understand the person's reasons for change,

412
00:19:38.120 --> 00:19:40.480
and what would lead them to consider shifting

413
00:19:40.480 --> 00:19:41.660
their opinion a little bit,

414
00:19:41.660 --> 00:19:43.313
then you might surface information

415
00:19:43.313 --> 00:19:45.613
that would lead them to change their own mind.

416
00:19:46.495 --> 00:19:48.320
Right, so let's dig into that a little bit deeper,

417
00:19:48.320 --> 00:19:50.510
but maybe we can start with elaborating

418
00:19:50.510 --> 00:19:52.820
a little bit more on this prosecutor,

419
00:19:52.820 --> 00:19:57.280
preacher, politician, rubric that you've set up here.

420
00:19:57.280 --> 00:19:58.750
The original idea comes from a colleague of mine,

421
00:19:58.750 --> 00:20:02.220
Phil Tetlock, who's studied political psychology

422
00:20:02.220 --> 00:20:04.510
and social psychology most of its career.

423
00:20:04.510 --> 00:20:08.330
And Phil observed that in decision making, in judgment,

424
00:20:08.330 --> 00:20:10.230
we spend a lot of time thinking like preachers,

425
00:20:10.230 --> 00:20:11.823
prosecutors and politicians.

426
00:20:12.980 --> 00:20:14.910
And we're usually not aware of it when we're doing it.

427
00:20:14.910 --> 00:20:18.120
A preacher mindset is essentially believing

428
00:20:18.120 --> 00:20:20.070
that you've already seen the light,

429
00:20:20.070 --> 00:20:22.160
and now you have to go and proselytize

430
00:20:22.160 --> 00:20:24.460
to everybody else and get them on board.

431
00:20:24.460 --> 00:20:26.890
So you've drunk the intellectual Kool-Aid,

432
00:20:26.890 --> 00:20:28.543
and now your job is to serve it.

433
00:20:29.470 --> 00:20:32.120
A prosecuting mindset is more about,

434
00:20:32.120 --> 00:20:33.390
proving everyone else wrong,

435
00:20:33.390 --> 00:20:35.940
and winning your side of the case.

436
00:20:35.940 --> 00:20:39.907
And then a politician mindset is really about saying,

437
00:20:39.907 --> 00:20:42.020
"Okay, I've got some constituents here,

438
00:20:42.020 --> 00:20:45.940
and I need to lobby for their buy in, I want their support."

439
00:20:45.940 --> 00:20:48.610
And my big worry Rich was when I learned

440
00:20:48.610 --> 00:20:49.750
about this framework, I thought,

441
00:20:49.750 --> 00:20:52.063
okay, preaching and prosecuting,

442
00:20:53.066 --> 00:20:55.100
those stop you from thinking again.

443
00:20:55.100 --> 00:20:58.060
Because if I know I'm right, and I'm sure you're wrong,

444
00:20:58.060 --> 00:21:00.990
I don't need to budge in any of my opinions

445
00:21:00.990 --> 00:21:03.940
or any of the knowledge that I hold,

446
00:21:03.940 --> 00:21:06.370
I just need to convince you to do that.

447
00:21:06.370 --> 00:21:07.790
And in politician mode,

448
00:21:07.790 --> 00:21:10.450
we see people sounding a lot more flexible,

