But I think one of the things that diving,

764
00:36:21.840 --> 00:36:23.410
I don't know if I learned it in diving,

765
00:36:23.410 --> 00:36:27.770
or if it just reinforced the value of it for me,

766
00:36:27.770 --> 00:36:32.770
and then it became more hardwired, was just the value

767
00:36:34.040 --> 00:36:36.570
of focusing on one thing at a time.

768
00:36:36.570 --> 00:36:40.000
The number of people I know who are multi-taskers,

769
00:36:40.000 --> 00:36:43.310
not necessarily even in the minute, but in the day,

770
00:36:43.310 --> 00:36:44.960
is much greater than I would like it to be.

771
00:36:44.960 --> 00:36:47.960
I think that computers are great at parallel processing.

772
00:36:47.960 --> 00:36:51.140
As humans, we are built to be serial processors.

773
00:36:51.140 --> 00:36:54.120
And I don't just mean that, if I'm checking my phone,

774
00:36:54.120 --> 00:36:57.800
while we're talking that I'm not gonna be coherent, I mean,

775
00:36:57.800 --> 00:37:00.440
that if I'm trying to accomplish two things today,

776
00:37:00.440 --> 00:37:02.630
I'm gonna do both of those less effectively,

777
00:37:02.630 --> 00:37:04.610
than if I just have one goal today.

778
00:37:04.610 --> 00:37:07.690
And that was what diving really was, it was,

779
00:37:07.690 --> 00:37:08.940
yeah, I would go to practice.

780
00:37:08.940 --> 00:37:12.450
And I would max out, usually it was two and a half

781
00:37:12.450 --> 00:37:16.140
or three hours before I start to get blisters on my feet.

782
00:37:16.140 --> 00:37:19.330
Or, you know, from repeated days of practice, or,

783
00:37:19.330 --> 00:37:21.690
you know, my shoulders would start to hurt or,

784
00:37:21.690 --> 00:37:23.160
you know, my form would just start to fall apart,

785
00:37:23.160 --> 00:37:24.730
and I needed to take a break.

786
00:37:24.730 --> 00:37:27.800
But then it meant I'm studying the video of my own practice,

787
00:37:27.800 --> 00:37:30.450
to really internalize what my coach is telling me.

788
00:37:30.450 --> 00:37:33.350
I'm analyzing what changes I might wanna make

789
00:37:33.350 --> 00:37:35.590
that my coaches didn't see.

790
00:37:35.590 --> 00:37:37.600
I'm heading over to a trampoline to practice some

791
00:37:37.600 --> 00:37:39.760
of the techniques that don't require water.

792
00:37:39.760 --> 00:37:41.260
I'm working on my stretching.

793
00:37:41.260 --> 00:37:44.070
And that single mindedness was I think,

794
00:37:44.070 --> 00:37:46.260
other than having incredible coaches,

795
00:37:46.260 --> 00:37:48.660
was the only reason that I got good at diving.

796
00:37:48.660 --> 00:37:53.610
And it's just, it's kind of, it's startling to me,

797
00:37:53.610 --> 00:37:58.140
it's, startling is the wrong word, it's mesmerizing to me,

798
00:37:58.140 --> 00:38:00.980
that people think you can be good at anything,

799
00:38:00.980 --> 00:38:03.320
when you're trying to be good at multiple things.

800
00:38:03.320 --> 00:38:06.100
And I'm not saying you can't master multiple things

801
00:38:06.100 --> 00:38:08.930
in a year or even in a month.

802
00:38:08.930 --> 00:38:12.280
But what I wanna do is, I wanna have not work life balance,

803
00:38:12.280 --> 00:38:16.870
or even project project balance, I wanna have rhythm,

804
00:38:16.870 --> 00:38:19.610
which means you know, today I've got one song

805
00:38:19.610 --> 00:38:20.670
that I'm playing or better yet,

806
00:38:20.670 --> 00:38:22.640
I've got one verse of that song.

807
00:38:22.640 --> 00:38:25.470
And then tomorrow, we'll go over to the course.

808
00:38:25.470 --> 00:38:27.910
Right, I mean that dovetails pretty nicely

809
00:38:27.910 --> 00:38:29.210
into this idea that you have

810
00:38:29.210 --> 00:38:32.690
about attention management versus time management,

811
00:38:32.690 --> 00:38:37.690
and your ability to be productive is calibrated

812
00:38:38.030 --> 00:38:43.030
with your ability to really invest your attention

813
00:38:43.590 --> 00:38:45.713
and your focus on one thing at a time.

814
00:38:46.680 --> 00:38:49.510
Yeah, I think that my big frustration with time management

815
00:38:49.510 --> 00:38:53.200
is that the more you try to optimize your calendar,

816
00:38:53.200 --> 00:38:55.730
the more aware you become of how many hours you waste

817
00:38:55.730 --> 00:38:56.960
in the day in the week.

818
00:38:56.960 --> 00:39:00.540
And that just is, it's discouraging and deflating to me.

819
00:39:00.540 --> 00:39:03.440
And I decided at some point that what I wanted to do

820
00:39:03.440 --> 00:39:05.830
was get better at managing my attention.

821
00:39:05.830 --> 00:39:07.890
And that meant that I had to have clarity

822
00:39:07.890 --> 00:39:10.390
about the people and the projects that mattered to me.

823
00:39:10.390 --> 00:39:12.760
And then if I was laser focused on those,

824
00:39:12.760 --> 00:39:14.870
it wouldn't matter how long anything took,

825
00:39:14.870 --> 00:39:18.223
because my time is aligned with my values and my goals.

826
00:39:19.180 --> 00:39:23.093
And I think that, in some cases, that just means,

827
00:39:25.680 --> 00:39:29.680
well, now I guess what I would say is,

828
00:39:29.680 --> 00:39:32.190
attention management sometimes gets me in trouble,

829
00:39:32.190 --> 00:39:34.910
because my attentional filters are so high,

830
00:39:34.910 --> 00:39:37.380
that my peripheral vision gets gone.

831
00:39:37.380 --> 00:39:41.440
And if I'm locked into a goal of finishing a task today,

832
00:39:41.440 --> 00:39:43.780
let's say I'm writing an op-ed,

833
00:39:43.780 --> 00:39:45.920
like that "New York Times," one that you referenced,

834
00:39:45.920 --> 00:39:49.120
I set a goal a few weeks ago of finishing a draft of it.

835
00:39:49.120 --> 00:39:53.860
And I was late to two meetings, I've missed one deadline,

836
00:39:53.860 --> 00:39:56.370
and I think I'm lucky to be in a job

837
00:39:56.370 --> 00:39:59.670
where I have the freedom to set my own deliverables.

838
00:39:59.670 --> 00:40:02.070
And I've also let everyone I know who works with me,

839
00:40:02.070 --> 00:40:04.300
know that one of my biggest vices is,

840
00:40:04.300 --> 00:40:08.030
that I have a chronic inability to disengage from a task,

841
00:40:08.030 --> 00:40:09.820
until it's done to my satisfaction,

842
00:40:09.820 --> 00:40:12.230
or at least until I've made real progress on it.

843
00:40:12.230 --> 00:40:16.390
Because I think those moments of flow, of deep work are,

844
00:40:16.390 --> 00:40:18.180
I don't wanna say they're rare, but they're precious,

845
00:40:18.180 --> 00:40:19.150
they need to be protected.

846
00:40:19.150 --> 00:40:22.580
And I wanna get every ounce of creativity and energy

847
00:40:22.580 --> 00:40:24.940
out of them when I get into them.

848
00:40:24.940 --> 00:40:26.920
Yeah, I mean, you can't task switch

849
00:40:26.920 --> 00:40:29.280
when you're in that process of creation.

850
00:40:29.280 --> 00:40:34.280
And there is a real qualitative difference between makers

851
00:40:34.800 --> 00:40:39.390
and managers, like makers really need to protect that time

852
00:40:39.390 --> 00:40:41.760
to do the thing that they do.

853
00:40:41.760 --> 00:40:45.400
And you can't toggle in between tasks

854
00:40:45.400 --> 00:40:48.130
when you're in the process of trying to make something.

855
00:40:48.130 --> 00:40:51.480
And so it's not surprising that it would compel you

856
00:40:51.480 --> 00:40:53.100
to be late or miss other things,

857
00:40:53.100 --> 00:40:57.400
because the priority has to go towards taking advantage

858
00:40:57.400 --> 00:41:00.320
of that flow when it descends upon you.

859
00:41:00.320 --> 00:41:02.970
Whereas the manager mindset is very different.

860
00:41:02.970 --> 00:41:05.020
They're task switching all the time,

861
00:41:05.020 --> 00:41:07.810
and it's about their interactions with lots of people

862
00:41:07.810 --> 00:41:09.400
and lots of emails and meetings,

863
00:41:09.400 --> 00:41:12.530
and all of that kind of stuff, which are really,

864
00:41:12.530 --> 00:41:15.470
just massive distractions to anybody

865
00:41:15.470 --> 00:41:19.350
who is more of a maker or creator.

866
00:41:19.350 --> 00:41:21.750
Yes, and I think this is one of the opportunities

867
00:41:21.750 --> 00:41:24.480
in front of us now, with remote work becoming very real

868
00:41:24.480 --> 00:41:27.330
for millions, if not hundreds of millions

869
00:41:27.330 --> 00:41:28.563
or billions of people.

870
00:41:29.499 --> 00:41:32.770
I keep thinking of this Leslie Parla experiment,

871
00:41:32.770 --> 00:41:37.310
where she said, "What if we just give people quiet time,

872
00:41:37.310 --> 00:41:40.170
three mornings a week, where they're told

873
00:41:40.170 --> 00:41:42.770
don't schedule meetings, no interruptions,

874
00:41:42.770 --> 00:41:45.580
you can actually just concentrate on focused work?"

875
00:41:45.580 --> 00:41:48.120
And she did this at a Fortune 500 company in India

876
00:41:48.120 --> 00:41:50.480
with engineers, if people were encouraged

877
00:41:50.480 --> 00:41:54.950
to do that themselves, 47% above average productivity.

878
00:41:54.950 --> 00:41:57.170
If the organization instituted it as a policy,

879
00:41:57.170 --> 00:41:59.210
it said, "Look, we mean this Tuesday, Thursday,

880
00:41:59.210 --> 00:42:02.500
Friday, no meetings, no interruptions before noon,"

881
00:42:02.500 --> 00:42:05.840
65% above average productivity.

882
00:42:05.840 --> 00:42:07.750
And if you break that down, what's going on there?

883
00:42:07.750 --> 00:42:09.540
Well, some of it is you are just benefiting

884
00:42:09.540 --> 00:42:11.750
from more flow and more deep work.

885
00:42:11.750 --> 00:42:13.960
But some of it is also just plain efficiency

886
00:42:13.960 --> 00:42:15.870
that you check your inbox after lunch,

887
00:42:15.870 --> 00:42:17.340
having had a productive morning.

888
00:42:17.340 --> 00:42:20.380
And you realize that six people had the same question.

889
00:42:20.380 --> 00:42:23.080
And now instead of having six different conversations,

890
00:42:23.080 --> 00:42:25.520
you can call all those people together in one meeting,

891
00:42:25.520 --> 00:42:27.360
it only takes one hour out of your day,

892
00:42:27.360 --> 00:42:29.770
and then you've also helped those people build a network

893
00:42:29.770 --> 00:42:32.730
of acquaintances, or maybe even strangers who didn't work

894
00:42:32.730 --> 00:42:35.100
realize that they were working on similar problems.

895
00:42:35.100 --> 00:42:38.407
And I've watched a lot of organizations just say,

896
00:42:38.407 --> 00:42:41.020
"You know what, we can't protect people's time,

897
00:42:41.020 --> 00:42:43.640
because we have customers or we have clients

898
00:42:44.620 --> 00:42:46.290
that impose demands on us."

899
00:42:46.290 --> 00:42:48.637
And my response to them has been,

900
00:42:48.637 --> 00:42:50.560
"Well, actually, don't you think this is how you teach

901
00:42:50.560 --> 00:42:53.280
your clients to be more effective and productive too?

902
00:42:53.280 --> 00:42:56.140
To say, look, we are here to be available to you

903
00:42:56.140 --> 00:42:58.060
and helpful to you wherever we can.

904
00:42:58.060 --> 00:43:00.420
And in order to do the best job,

905
00:43:00.420 --> 00:43:02.190
not only reacting to your problems,

906
00:43:02.190 --> 00:43:03.950
and fighting every fire that comes up,

907
00:43:03.950 --> 00:43:06.730
but also really rethinking how we solve problems

908
00:43:06.730 --> 00:43:09.080
in the first place or better yet how to prevent problems

909
00:43:09.080 --> 00:43:11.510
at scale, we need some protected time.

910
00:43:11.510 --> 00:43:13.120
And so of course, we're happy to hear

911
00:43:13.120 --> 00:43:14.650
from you if it's an emergency.

912
00:43:14.650 --> 00:43:16.680
But we're gonna have one day a week

913
00:43:16.680 --> 00:43:18.530
where we don't schedule meetings,

914
00:43:18.530 --> 00:43:20.530
or we're gonna have a couple mornings a week,

915
00:43:20.530 --> 00:43:22.030
where we don't take calls.

916
00:43:22.030 --> 00:43:25.596
And we want you to know, that's part of how we serve you."

917
00:43:25.596 --> 00:43:28.540
It's interesting, I mean, it's counterintuitive

918
00:43:28.540 --> 00:43:30.960
in the sense that from a managerial perspective,

919
00:43:30.960 --> 00:43:33.450
you would think that people would then just use that time

920
00:43:33.450 --> 00:43:35.470
to go fuck off and do whatever they want,

921
00:43:35.470 --> 00:43:37.590
they're not gonna actually use that

922
00:43:37.590 --> 00:43:41.290
for some kind of deep work, they're gonna go, you know,

923
00:43:41.290 --> 00:43:44.320
I don't know, go run errands or something like that.

924
00:43:44.320 --> 00:43:45.760
Well, if you're a bad manager,

925
00:43:45.760 --> 00:43:48.203
that's probably what they will do.

926
00:43:48.203 --> 00:43:50.640
Because you haven't earned any trust or loyalty.

927
00:43:50.640 --> 00:43:55.180
But if you're a manager who instead of being a micromanager,

928
00:43:55.180 --> 00:43:57.207
is a macro manager, who says,

929
00:43:57.207 --> 00:43:59.760
"My job is to figure out the purpose

930
00:43:59.760 --> 00:44:02.690
and the mission of the team in the organization,

931
00:44:02.690 --> 00:44:06.190
and then help you identify your unique individual role

932
00:44:06.190 --> 00:44:08.589
and see your contribution here.

933
00:44:08.589 --> 00:44:10.500
And then I'm gonna get out of the way and not tell you

934
00:44:10.500 --> 00:44:13.090
how to do everything or when to do everything."

935
00:44:13.090 --> 00:44:15.430
You get a lot of intrinsic motivation in response to that.

936
00:44:15.430 --> 00:44:18.370
And we've got, at this point, almost half a century

937
00:44:18.370 --> 00:44:20.290
of evidence on the benefits

938
00:44:20.290 --> 00:44:22.650
of what I guess classically Douglas McGregor

939
00:44:22.650 --> 00:44:25.700
would have called a Theory Y As opposed to Theory X approach

940
00:44:25.700 --> 00:44:28.880
where, you know, whether you believe that people are lazy

941
00:44:28.880 --> 00:44:32.360
and can't be trusted, or people are intrinsically motivated

942
00:44:32.360 --> 00:44:35.100
by mastery and learning and purpose,

943
00:44:35.100 --> 00:44:37.380
those beliefs often become self fulfilling prophecies

944
00:44:37.380 --> 00:44:39.370
and you get exactly what you expected.

945
00:44:39.370 --> 00:44:41.670
Yeah, interesting, well, as somebody

946
00:44:41.670 --> 00:44:45.360
who is spent their entire career kind of devoted

947
00:44:45.360 --> 00:44:49.340
to better understanding the psychology of the workplace,

948
00:44:49.340 --> 00:44:53.740
this past year has to be, you know, fascinating

949
00:44:53.740 --> 00:44:58.740
for you to see the ascent of the home office and you know,

950
00:44:58.790 --> 00:45:01.800
everything that COVID has has brought us,

951
00:45:01.800 --> 00:45:04.200
and the disruptions in how we work.

952
00:45:04.200 --> 00:45:07.330
So what are some of the insights that you've been able

953
00:45:07.330 --> 00:45:09.260
to develop out of this, you know,

954
00:45:09.260 --> 00:45:13.490
very strange and unique year that we've just experienced?

955
00:45:13.490 --> 00:45:14.930
There have been a lot.

956
00:45:14.930 --> 00:45:16.750
Not surprisingly, this is the experiment

957
00:45:16.750 --> 00:45:18.170
that nobody opted into.

958
00:45:18.170 --> 00:45:19.660
And since we're stuck with it,

959
00:45:19.660 --> 00:45:21.870
we might as well be learning from it.

960
00:45:21.870 --> 00:45:25.207
I think what are my first, I guess,

961
00:45:25.207 --> 00:45:28.690
one of the first revelations for me was Ethan Bernstein,

962
00:45:28.690 --> 00:45:32.740
and Hayley Blunden collected data on just March through May,

963
00:45:32.740 --> 00:45:36.370
how is communication changing, now that essentially

964
00:45:36.370 --> 00:45:39.530
everybody who could, is working remotely?

965
00:45:39.530 --> 00:45:41.850
And they found that communication with strong ties went up

966
00:45:41.850 --> 00:45:45.650
about 40% on average, that with your closest colleagues,

967
00:45:45.650 --> 00:45:47.310
your boss, your direct reports,

968
00:45:47.310 --> 00:45:49.660
the clients that you're in touch with regularly,

969
00:45:49.660 --> 00:45:51.590
you knew you weren't just gonna run into them,

970
00:45:51.590 --> 00:45:53.610
or you weren't flying out to see them,

971
00:45:53.610 --> 00:45:56.620
and you worked hard to schedule extra opportunities

972
00:45:56.620 --> 00:45:58.483
to connect with them, usually over Zoom.

973
00:46:00.060 --> 00:46:01.870
Communication with weak ties, though,

974
00:46:01.870 --> 00:46:03.270
your more distant acquaintances,

975
00:46:03.270 --> 00:46:06.820
your suppliers, the mentor you occasionally talk to,

976
00:46:06.820 --> 00:46:09.440
that was actually down 10%.

977
00:46:09.440 --> 00:46:12.890
And this is bad news when it comes to rethinking things,

978
00:46:12.890 --> 00:46:14.550
getting a fresh perspective.

979
00:46:14.550 --> 00:46:15.800
Because weak ties tend to give us

980
00:46:15.800 --> 00:46:18.400
more novel information than strong ties.

981
00:46:18.400 --> 00:46:20.530
Strong ties tend to know the same people in the same stuff

982
00:46:20.530 --> 00:46:23.670
that you do, they give you a redundant knowledge.

983
00:46:23.670 --> 00:46:25.440
weak ties are traveling different circles,

984
00:46:25.440 --> 00:46:26.510
they're meeting different people,

985
00:46:26.510 --> 00:46:27.830
they're learning different things.

986
00:46:27.830 --> 00:46:30.160
So they're much more likely to open up fresh access

987
00:46:30.160 --> 00:46:33.470
to a creative idea, and because we're not having

988
00:46:33.470 --> 00:46:35.760
those spontaneous creative collisions, like we used to

989
00:46:35.760 --> 00:46:38.200
before the pandemic, I think one of the things we have

990
00:46:38.200 --> 00:46:40.900
to figure out is how do we engineer interactions

991
00:46:40.900 --> 00:46:43.880
between people and their more distant acquaintances,

992
00:46:43.880 --> 00:46:46.490
or allow them even to learn from strangers?

993
00:46:46.490 --> 00:46:49.470
And I don't think we have good,

994
00:46:49.470 --> 00:46:52.440
I don't think we have good practices yet to figure that out.

995
00:46:52.440 --> 00:46:54.090
How have you navigated that Rich?

996
00:46:55.150 --> 00:46:57.800
Well, I'm very lucky in the sense that I've been able

997
00:46:57.800 --> 00:47:00.230
to continue to pursue my living

998
00:47:00.230 --> 00:47:03.330
where a lot of people are not so lucky,

999
00:47:03.330 --> 00:47:05.500
so I feel extremely grateful.

1000
00:47:05.500 --> 00:47:08.610
A big part of the show that I do,

1001
00:47:08.610 --> 00:47:12.400
which is the the gravamen of my vocation,

1002
00:47:12.400 --> 00:47:15.680
is sitting across from another individual live

1003
00:47:15.680 --> 00:47:16.910
and doing it in person.

1004
00:47:16.910 --> 00:47:19.490
And, you know, we've set up safety protocols,

1005
00:47:19.490 --> 00:47:22.090
and I've been able to do that to some extent, but you know,

1006
00:47:22.090 --> 00:47:24.400
like, what we're doing right now, some of them

1007
00:47:24.400 --> 00:47:28.610
have been forced to be, you know, on a digital platform,

1008
00:47:28.610 --> 00:47:29.730
which is not my preference.

1009
00:47:29.730 --> 00:47:33.390
Like, for me, this experience is all about,

1010
00:47:33.390 --> 00:47:35.580
like trying to connect as deeply as possible

1011
00:47:35.580 --> 00:47:37.380
with the person that I'm talking to.

1012
00:47:38.330 --> 00:47:41.860
And very often these people become part of my life,

1013
00:47:41.860 --> 00:47:43.970
maybe they become part of my challenge network,

1014
00:47:43.970 --> 00:47:45.560
or my support network,

1015
00:47:45.560 --> 00:47:48.750
or my board of advisors, or just friends.

1016
00:47:48.750 --> 00:47:52.480
And that's incredibly valuable to me,

1017
00:47:52.480 --> 00:47:55.040
I mean, it's a gift to be able to share, you know,

1018
00:47:55.040 --> 00:47:58.720
the wisdom of people like yourself, with so many people.

1019
00:47:58.720 --> 00:48:02.130
But the enriching aspect of it

1020
00:48:02.130 --> 00:48:05.140
for me is the in person connection.

1021
00:48:05.140 --> 00:48:08.610
And my kind of personal strategy

1022
00:48:08.610 --> 00:48:10.440
with all of these conversations

1023
00:48:10.440 --> 00:48:13.280
is that the emotional connection comes first.

1024
00:48:13.280 --> 00:48:15.830
It's less about the information, like I trust

1025
00:48:15.830 --> 00:48:18.010
that if I can connect with the person who's sitting across

1026
00:48:18.010 --> 00:48:22.150
from me, find a way in to really identify with them,

1027
00:48:22.150 --> 00:48:23.550
that whatever information needs

1028
00:48:23.550 --> 00:48:25.540
to be imparted will be imparted,

1029
00:48:25.540 --> 00:48:28.720
and I just try to be as present as possible for that,

1030
00:48:28.720 --> 00:48:31.610
and I'm able to achieve that, to some extent,

1031
00:48:31.610 --> 00:48:35.330
when I'm looking at you on a screen, but it's not the same.

1032
00:48:35.330 --> 00:48:37.830
Well, it still comes through loud and clear to me.

1033
00:48:37.830 --> 00:48:40.770
I can feel it, I can see it, I can hear it.

1034
00:48:40.770 --> 00:48:43.232
I should say first of all, I would love to be friends.

1035
00:48:43.232 --> 00:48:45.197
In case that's on your goal list already.

1036
00:48:45.197 --> 00:48:47.060
Okay I get it, I'm gonna call you my friend now.

1037
00:48:47.060 --> 00:48:50.223
Sorry, I mean, we're friends, consider it done.

1038
00:48:52.492 --> 00:48:54.610
I mean, I felt, coming in I felt like I knew you already

1039
00:48:54.610 --> 00:48:56.960
because I've followed your work with such great interest.

1040
00:48:56.960 --> 00:48:58.527
And it's so clear what you stand for

1041
00:48:58.527 --> 00:49:02.230
and what your values are, that I felt like I knew you.

1042
00:49:02.230 --> 00:49:03.760
So there was that connection,

1043
00:49:03.760 --> 00:49:05.720
even if swimmers and divers are supposed

1044
00:49:05.720 --> 00:49:07.723
to not get along, but I felt coming in that--

1045
00:49:07.723 --> 00:49:10.100
It's the weirdest pairing of two sports

1046
00:49:10.100 --> 00:49:12.230
into one that it shouldn't exist.

1047
00:49:12.230 --> 00:49:14.010
I'm Malcolm (indistinct) would have

1048
00:49:14.010 --> 00:49:14.977
a lot of opinions on that.

1049
00:49:14.977 --> 00:49:15.810
(Rich laughs)

1050
00:49:15.810 --> 00:49:17.610
It's like, well, these two things both involve water.

1051
00:49:17.610 --> 00:49:19.460
let's put them on a team together.

1052
00:49:19.460 --> 00:49:22.070
I guess there's also a masochistic element of both though,

1053
00:49:22.070 --> 00:49:27.070
which is, I think both of us opted into more deliberate pain

1054
00:49:27.140 --> 00:49:28.883
than many sports require.

1055
00:49:29.730 --> 00:49:33.200
I don't remember basketball or soccer ever hurting nearly

1056
00:49:33.200 --> 00:49:34.900
as much as diving did.

1057
00:49:34.900 --> 00:49:37.490
Yeah, swimming is a glutton for people

1058
00:49:37.490 --> 00:49:40.380
who enjoy self punishment, I can tell that.

1059
00:49:40.380 --> 00:49:42.333
But Rich, I did wanna ask you.

1060
00:49:43.516 --> 00:49:45.480
I've been curious about, yeah,

1061
00:49:45.480 --> 00:49:48.500
there's just, I think we're gonna have a wealth

1062
00:49:48.500 --> 00:49:51.430
of data in the next few years about in person

1063
00:49:51.430 --> 00:49:54.260
versus video, versus audio interactions.

1064
00:49:54.260 --> 00:49:58.190
And one thing I have noticed, I'm also drawn to the rapport

1065
00:49:58.190 --> 00:50:01.280
and the connection, and I've always been the person

1066
00:50:01.280 --> 00:50:02.890
who if I have a choice, I wanna sit down

1067
00:50:02.890 --> 00:50:05.450
in a room with someone because I can,

1068
00:50:05.450 --> 00:50:07.160
I feel like I can bond with him more.

1069
00:50:07.160 --> 00:50:11.260
I also, it's easier to get into that burstiness of you know,

1070
00:50:11.260 --> 00:50:15.083
we're just, we have this energy flying back and forth,

1071
00:50:15.083 --> 00:50:18.210
and the conversation is literally bursting with ideas.

1072
00:50:18.210 --> 00:50:22.720
I have noticed though, in the past few years of podcasting,

1073
00:50:22.720 --> 00:50:25.370
that I think I have deeper conversations sometimes

1074
00:50:25.370 --> 00:50:26.880
when I'm not in the room with people.

1075
00:50:26.880 --> 00:50:30.680
And it's because I'm less attuned to this question of, okay,

1076
00:50:30.680 --> 00:50:32.920
how did they react to what I just said?

1077
00:50:32.920 --> 00:50:36.470
Do they like me, you know, or was that awkward,

1078
00:50:36.470 --> 00:50:38.270
or did it build rapport?

1079
00:50:38.270 --> 00:50:41.340
And I'm much more comfortable with an awkward pause.

1080
00:50:41.340 --> 00:50:44.070
I'm much more interested in trying to figure out,

1081
00:50:44.070 --> 00:50:45.770
where's this conversation gonna take us?

1082
00:50:45.770 --> 00:50:49.370
And so I wonder if there's a purity of discussion,

1083
00:50:49.370 --> 00:50:52.663
that comes from distance, what has that been like for you?

1084
00:50:53.780 --> 00:50:55.223
That's very interesting.

1085
00:50:58.270 --> 00:51:00.350
There's certainly some truth in that.

1086
00:51:00.350 --> 00:51:02.090
The question that popped into my head

1087
00:51:02.090 --> 00:51:03.280
when you were describing that,

1088
00:51:03.280 --> 00:51:04.730
is whether there's a difference

1089
00:51:04.730 --> 00:51:08.780
between when you're doing it on Zoom, and you're looking

1090
00:51:08.780 --> 00:51:12.270
at the person versus an audio only platform

1091
00:51:12.270 --> 00:51:15.300
where you don't have to worry about like,

1092
00:51:15.300 --> 00:51:16.830
I don't wanna look down at my notes,

1093
00:51:16.830 --> 00:51:18.490
I wanna maintain eye contact with you.

1094
00:51:18.490 --> 00:51:20.960
But if we were doing this on a different platform,

1095
00:51:20.960 --> 00:51:22.940
where we weren't doing video, and I could be staring

1096
00:51:22.940 --> 00:51:27.340
at my notes, maybe I would have better questions chambered,

1097
00:51:27.340 --> 00:51:29.990
but I also wouldn't be as fully present

1098
00:51:29.990 --> 00:51:31.950
for the experience that's happening.

1099
00:51:31.950 --> 00:51:33.380
Yeah, there's a trade off there.

1100
00:51:33.380 --> 00:51:36.890
Yeah, so I don't know, and to kind of answer

1101
00:51:36.890 --> 00:51:39.170
the other aspect of your question, you know,

1102
00:51:39.170 --> 00:51:40.410
how is this experience bad,

1103
00:51:40.410 --> 00:51:43.130
I mean outside of the podcast itself,

1104
00:51:43.130 --> 00:51:45.370
I went into this pandemic thinking,

1105
00:51:45.370 --> 00:51:47.110
I've been training for this my whole life,

1106
00:51:47.110 --> 00:51:50.470
like I'm an introvert, I like my solitary time,

1107
00:51:50.470 --> 00:51:53.010
this is gonna be just fine, I'll be able to cancel

1108
00:51:53.010 --> 00:51:54.250
all these things on my calendar

1109
00:51:54.250 --> 00:51:56.020
that I didn't really wanna do anyway,

1110
00:51:56.020 --> 00:51:59.610
and focus on some deep work and just being with my family,

1111
00:51:59.610 --> 00:52:01.950
but none of us would have expected

1112
00:52:01.950 --> 00:52:03.610
that it would be going on this long.

1113
00:52:03.610 --> 00:52:07.210
And even you know, despite those predispositions

1114
00:52:07.210 --> 00:52:10.240
that I have, I do feel lonely, and I do miss my friends.

1115
00:52:10.240 --> 00:52:12.450
And I find myself yearning

1116
00:52:12.450 --> 00:52:14.700
for that interpersonal connection.

1117
00:52:14.700 --> 00:52:18.650
And I feel terrible for my kids who are being deprived

1118
00:52:18.650 --> 00:52:22.070
of experiences that, you know, no child should be deprived

1119
00:52:22.070 --> 00:52:24.670
of in terms of their ability to socially interact

1120
00:52:24.670 --> 00:52:25.790
with their peers.

1121
00:52:25.790 --> 00:52:28.290
Yeah, well first of all, I'm sorry to hear that,

1122
00:52:28.290 --> 00:52:31.410
I think I can relate to so much of that.

1123
00:52:31.410 --> 00:52:34.640
And I think everyone assumed going in that introverts

1124
00:52:34.640 --> 00:52:36.580
are gonna have an easier time with this than extroverts,

1125
00:52:36.580 --> 00:52:40.110
and this is another Bernstein, Blunden at all finding,

1126
00:52:40.110 --> 00:52:42.460
there was no difference in the well being of introverts

1127
00:52:42.460 --> 00:52:45.987
and extroverts during the early stages of the pandemic.

1128
00:52:45.987 --> 00:52:46.820
And I think that's in part

1129
00:52:46.820 --> 00:52:49.290
because everyone craves connection.

1130
00:52:49.290 --> 00:52:52.260
Even those of us who are introverts, you're one I'm one,

1131
00:52:52.260 --> 00:52:55.130
we are energized by interactions with other people.

1132
00:52:55.130 --> 00:52:57.110
We're also more likely to be overstimulated

1133
00:52:57.110 --> 00:52:58.710
by too much of that interaction.

1134
00:52:59.684 --> 00:53:01.610
And I think that's where the physiological difference

1135
00:53:01.610 --> 00:53:03.520
between extroverts and introverts lies,

1136
00:53:03.520 --> 00:53:06.510
according to the science there but,

1137
00:53:06.510 --> 00:53:09.400
I think that the loneliness piece

1138
00:53:09.400 --> 00:53:11.240
of this is real for a lot of people.

1139
00:53:11.240 --> 00:53:16.240
And I wonder, I think one thing I've started to rethink,

1140
00:53:17.400 --> 00:53:18.990
and I really underestimated,

1141
00:53:18.990 --> 00:53:23.930
is just how much casual interaction matters in my life.

1142
00:53:23.930 --> 00:53:27.930
I never thought just seeing somebody at a restaurant,

1143
00:53:27.930 --> 00:53:31.014
right without even talking to them necessarily.

1144
00:53:31.014 --> 00:53:32.650
And it never dawned on me that that was part

1145
00:53:32.650 --> 00:53:34.680
of feeling a sense of community,

1146
00:53:34.680 --> 00:53:38.640
or just deep belonging in a place.

1147
00:53:38.640 --> 00:53:41.640
And I think that, my hope is I'll appreciate that now,

1148
00:53:41.640 --> 00:53:43.780
in a way that I didn't before.

1149
00:53:43.780 --> 00:53:46.130
Well, in thinking about the workplace,

1150
00:53:46.130 --> 00:53:48.990
and what this year has taught us,

1151
00:53:48.990 --> 00:53:53.320
you talked a little bit about enhancements in productivity.

1152
00:53:53.320 --> 00:53:56.520
What do you think, what's your estimation of,

1153
00:53:56.520 --> 00:54:00.070
once we get back to our ability to safely interact,

1154
00:54:00.070 --> 00:54:03.860
like, what is gonna snap back to the way it was before,

1155
00:54:03.860 --> 00:54:04.693
and what's not?

1156
00:54:04.693 --> 00:54:06.600
I mean, we're seeing migrations

1157
00:54:06.600 --> 00:54:09.063
of people moving out of cities and finding,

1158
00:54:10.280 --> 00:54:13.100
places to live that are more affordable, etc.

1159
00:54:13.100 --> 00:54:16.940
So I think certainly, there's permanent implications

1160
00:54:16.940 --> 00:54:19.620
to this, where people are realizing they didn't need

1161
00:54:19.620 --> 00:54:21.920
to do a lot of the things they were doing before,

1162
00:54:21.920 --> 00:54:25.960
and yet, we do have to get back to some level

1163
00:54:25.960 --> 00:54:28.640
of healthy interpersonal interaction.

1164
00:54:28.640 --> 00:54:29.680
So what does that you know,

1165
00:54:29.680 --> 00:54:32.070
where's your thinking in terms of that?

1166
00:54:32.070 --> 00:54:33.400
I wish I had a crystal ball.

1167
00:54:33.400 --> 00:54:35.860
I think it's definitely still a mystery.

1168
00:54:35.860 --> 00:54:39.792
I can tell you, the data that I've seen so far

1169
00:54:39.792 --> 00:54:41.670
would predict a few things.

1170
00:54:41.670 --> 00:54:44.630
First one is Nick Blum and his colleagues just did

1171
00:54:44.630 --> 00:54:48.540
a nationally representative survey of 15,000 Americans.

1172
00:54:48.540 --> 00:54:51.640
And they found that essentially, people are expecting

1173
00:54:51.640 --> 00:54:54.990
to do usually somewhere between 20 and 25%

1174
00:54:54.990 --> 00:54:57.640
of their workdays remote, and that's taking

1175
00:54:57.640 --> 00:54:59.070
into account individual preferences

1176
00:54:59.070 --> 00:55:02.540
as well as the organic policies that are starting to emerge.

1177
00:55:02.540 --> 00:55:04.300
And most of that is hybrid.

1178
00:55:04.300 --> 00:55:06.587
That's not, you know, a bunch of companies saying,

1179
00:55:06.587 --> 00:55:08.460
"We're gonna be fully in the office,"

1180
00:55:08.460 --> 00:55:09.817
and a bunch of workplaces saying,

1181
00:55:09.817 --> 00:55:11.350
"We're gonna be fully remote."

1182
00:55:11.350 --> 00:55:13.717
That's most people and most leaders saying,

1183
00:55:13.717 --> 00:55:15.480
"We'd love to have people in the office three,

1184
00:55:15.480 --> 00:55:19.220
four days a week, and then work from anywhere the rest."

1185
00:55:19.220 --> 00:55:20.660
The challenge with that is,

1186
00:55:20.660 --> 00:55:22.970
what kinda office space do you need?

1187
00:55:22.970 --> 00:55:26.060
If you do you want everyone to be there three days,

1188
00:55:26.060 --> 00:55:28.860
then you need a huge office, and it's a major waste?

1189
00:55:28.860 --> 00:55:31.810
Are you gonna do shift work like a hospital,

1190
00:55:31.810 --> 00:55:34.270
where people come in at different times,

1191
00:55:34.270 --> 00:55:35.380
or even on different days?

1192
00:55:35.380 --> 00:55:38.640
Well, then you're missing the sense of cohesiveness

1193
00:55:38.640 --> 00:55:41.440
and a culture, I think those are the kinds of questions

1194
00:55:41.440 --> 00:55:43.920
that a lot of leaders are grappling with right now.

1195
00:55:43.920 --> 00:55:47.320
I think one thing we can be reasonably confident

1196
00:55:47.320 --> 00:55:52.080
in predicting is, we're not gonna see a return

1197
00:55:52.080 --> 00:55:55.300
to this giant hub, tiny spokes model,

1198
00:55:55.300 --> 00:55:57.590
that used to organize most workplaces, right?

1199
00:55:57.590 --> 00:56:00.390
where everybody's wanting to be at headquarters.

1200
00:56:00.390 --> 00:56:05.130
And then you see occasional people who are in satellites,

1201
00:56:05.130 --> 00:56:07.730
I think we're gonna see probably a much smaller hub,

1202
00:56:07.730 --> 00:56:10.970
and then more affordable places to work in different parts

1203
00:56:10.970 --> 00:56:12.460
of the country or different parts of the world

1204
00:56:12.460 --> 00:56:16.360
for a lot of organizations, but giant caveat on this is,

1205
00:56:16.360 --> 00:56:19.520
people wanna be where the most senior leader is.

1206
00:56:19.520 --> 00:56:21.410
They wanna be where they can get face time

1207
00:56:21.410 --> 00:56:22.670
with powerful people,

1208
00:56:22.670 --> 00:56:24.570
they wanna be where they can get mentored

1209
00:56:24.570 --> 00:56:27.330
by experienced experts,

1210
00:56:27.330 --> 00:56:30.530
and they wanna be where they think they can be part

1211
00:56:30.530 --> 00:56:32.820
of what is the real community.

1212
00:56:32.820 --> 00:56:36.380
And I don't know how, I don't see that going away.

1213
00:56:36.380 --> 00:56:38.360
And so I think we're still gonna end up with hubs,

1214
00:56:38.360 --> 00:56:39.890
they're just gonna be smaller hubs.

1215
00:56:39.890 --> 00:56:42.530
Right, it wasn't that long ago that the idea

1216
00:56:42.530 --> 00:56:44.960
of working from home was this huge benefit.

1217
00:56:44.960 --> 00:56:48.090
And now, the idea of getting to go into an office

1218
00:56:48.090 --> 00:56:49.440
just seem so appealing.

1219
00:56:49.440 --> 00:56:52.750
You know, it's very strange how the mind works around that.

1220
00:56:52.750 --> 00:56:54.520
But I would tend to agree with that.

1221
00:56:54.520 --> 00:56:56.900
I mean, I think it's gonna be fascinating to see.

1222
00:56:56.900 --> 00:57:00.040
And I'm wondering, do you have your finger on the pulse

1223
00:57:00.040 --> 00:57:05.040
of people's satisfaction with their vocation,

1224
00:57:05.410 --> 00:57:08.090
when they're working from home, like in this new situation,

1225
00:57:08.090 --> 00:57:10.170
like are people happier?

1226
00:57:10.170 --> 00:57:12.410
Like setting aside the other aspects of COVID?

1227
00:57:12.410 --> 00:57:15.847
Like, do they enjoy this more or are they waiting

1228
00:57:17.610 --> 00:57:19.230
until they can go back into the office,

1229
00:57:19.230 --> 00:57:21.030
like, where does that fall?

1230
00:57:21.030 --> 00:57:22.700
It's another giant question mark,

1231
00:57:22.700 --> 00:57:25.160
because we have our data pre pandemic,

1232
00:57:25.160 --> 00:57:27.240
when nobody had the same experience

1233
00:57:27.240 --> 00:57:28.950
working remotely but we do now.

1234
00:57:28.950 --> 00:57:30.580
And then we have data during the pandemic,

1235
00:57:30.580 --> 00:57:32.357
when we have all these confounds of you know,

1236
00:57:32.357 --> 00:57:34.170
"I'm afraid I might get COVID,

1237
00:57:34.170 --> 00:57:36.190
and there are limitations on what I can do

1238
00:57:36.190 --> 00:57:38.210
and who I can see."

1239
00:57:38.210 --> 00:57:39.655
My guess is though,

1240
00:57:39.655 --> 00:57:43.440
there's probably a net benefit to flexibility.

1241
00:57:43.440 --> 00:57:45.470
That doesn't mean you should be permanently remote, right?

1242
00:57:45.470 --> 00:57:48.150
But to have the choice to work from wherever you want,

1243
00:57:48.150 --> 00:57:50.973
and to do that, at least part of your time,

1244
00:57:50.973 --> 00:57:53.320
I think that seems to be good for well being

1245
00:57:53.320 --> 00:57:55.030
and the early data on this,

1246
00:57:55.030 --> 00:57:58.980
there was a meta analysis, Gajendran and Harrison, 2007,

1247
00:57:58.980 --> 00:58:01.010
they cumulated every study that had been done to date

1248
00:58:01.010 --> 00:58:03.090
on what was called telecommuting,

1249
00:58:03.090 --> 00:58:04.970
which sounds like a thing of the 1990s,

1250
00:58:04.970 --> 00:58:08.100
but they basically meant remote work.

1251
00:58:08.100 --> 00:58:11.420
And they found that as long as people came to work,

1252
00:58:11.420 --> 00:58:14.620
two and a half days a week, performance was higher,

1253
00:58:14.620 --> 00:58:17.210
satisfaction was higher, and there were no cost

1254
00:58:17.210 --> 00:58:20.120
to the quality of co worker relationships.

1255
00:58:20.120 --> 00:58:22.720
And I think that then tracks

1256
00:58:22.720 --> 00:58:24.380
with a lot of what we're seeing in the pandemic,

1257
00:58:24.380 --> 00:58:27.760
which is people don't like their jobs less on average,

1258
00:58:27.760 --> 00:58:29.670
when they're working remotely.

1259
00:58:29.670 --> 00:58:31.981
They found that they, there's some things they like better,

1260
00:58:31.981 --> 00:58:33.740
and there's some things they like worse,

1261
00:58:33.740 --> 00:58:37.010
I think the general upsides have been people love

1262
00:58:37.010 --> 00:58:38.810
the autonomy and freedom.

1263
00:58:38.810 --> 00:58:40.610
They appreciate not having to commute,

1264
00:58:40.610 --> 00:58:43.670
although some of them will choose to commute again.

1265
00:58:43.670 --> 00:58:47.070
They like also, you know, in some cases,

1266
00:58:47.070 --> 00:58:50.270
the fact that they get to choose just a work environment

1267
00:58:50.270 --> 00:58:51.570
where they're comfortable.

1268
00:58:52.500 --> 00:58:54.800
I think on the flip side, people miss the structure,

1269
00:58:54.800 --> 00:58:57.320
they miss the community, they miss the culture.

1270
00:58:57.320 --> 00:58:59.810
And I think we want all of those things,

1271
00:58:59.810 --> 00:59:01.870
so I think we're gonna mostly need to be hybrid

1272
00:59:01.870 --> 00:59:02.830
to get all those things.

1273
00:59:02.830 --> 00:59:05.670
Yeah, I mean, Zoom fatigue is real.

1274
00:59:05.670 --> 00:59:06.860
What's behind that for you?

1275
00:59:06.860 --> 00:59:08.800
Because I've been hearing a lot about this,

1276
00:59:08.800 --> 00:59:10.623
and I've felt it from time to time.

1277
00:59:11.520 --> 00:59:12.920
When you talk about Zoom fatigue,

1278
00:59:12.920 --> 00:59:15.120
what's contributing to that for you?

1279
00:59:15.120 --> 00:59:17.550
I'm not somebody who's on tons of Zoom calls,

1280
00:59:17.550 --> 00:59:20.610
so I might not be the best person to feel this question.

1281
00:59:20.610 --> 00:59:24.110
But I just, I don't like the static nature.

1282
00:59:24.110 --> 00:59:26.640
I don't like sitting still staring at a screen

1283
00:59:26.640 --> 00:59:29.460
when I don't know what it is, I can't put my finger on it,

1284
00:59:29.460 --> 00:59:31.900
but it makes me feel very restless.

1285
00:59:31.900 --> 00:59:34.233
Like, I just wanna get up and move around.

1286
00:59:35.150 --> 00:59:38.050
I don't know, I mean--
Got it, interesting.

1287
00:59:38.050 --> 00:59:40.830
I mean, it's got to be a thing that's,

1288
00:59:40.830 --> 00:59:43.643
not like it's gonna be in the DSM five or whatever.

1289
00:59:44.990 --> 00:59:47.400
But it's definitely a real condition.

1290
00:59:47.400 --> 00:59:49.100
I mean, what are some of the experiences

1291
00:59:49.100 --> 00:59:51.980
that people have shared with you about that?

1292
00:59:51.980 --> 00:59:54.025
What I'm seeing so far, yeah, for some people,

1293
00:59:54.025 --> 00:59:57.050
it's just, I'm too static, which it sounds

1294
00:59:57.050 --> 00:59:58.380
like it's your experience.

1295
00:59:58.380 --> 01:00:01.272
For some people, it's the exhaustion

1296
01:00:01.272 --> 01:00:03.960
of talking into a screen,

1297
01:00:03.960 --> 01:00:08.837
and trying to read people's facial cues or parse tone

1298
01:00:09.900 --> 01:00:11.810
in a way that's never quite as clear

1299
01:00:11.810 --> 01:00:14.050
as if you're actually in the same room.

1300
01:00:14.050 --> 01:00:17.290
For some people, it's a lack of a sense

1301
01:00:17.290 --> 01:00:19.313
of just being in the same place.

1302
01:00:20.230 --> 01:00:22.570
And you know, that's just interfering with connection.

1303
01:00:22.570 --> 01:00:25.250
And my guess is of all these factors,

1304
01:00:25.250 --> 01:00:28.410
just the sitting still staring at a screen,

1305
01:00:28.410 --> 01:00:31.810
that would probably be the biggest contributor to it.

1306
01:00:31.810 --> 01:00:33.870
But we have, a lot of people have the flexibility

1307
01:00:33.870 --> 01:00:34.810
to try to change that, right?

1308
01:00:34.810 --> 01:00:36.397
I've watched a lot of people say,

1309
01:00:36.397 --> 01:00:38.400
"Alright, I don't need to be on camera,

1310
01:00:38.400 --> 01:00:40.110
I'm doing walking meetings."

1311
01:00:40.110 --> 01:00:42.240
Or we've even agreed as a team

1312
01:00:42.240 --> 01:00:44.710
that we're gonna take a couple of our standing meetings,

1313
01:00:44.710 --> 01:00:47.330
and we're gonna make those, hey, you can do a workout,

1314
01:00:47.330 --> 01:00:49.270
it probably shouldn't be an intense workout,

1315
01:00:49.270 --> 01:00:51.915
you shouldn't go swimming, definitely, but,

1316
01:00:51.915 --> 01:00:52.748
you know, you can go for a walk,

1317
01:00:52.748 --> 01:00:54.720
or you can go for a light jog,

1318
01:00:54.720 --> 01:00:58.430
or even play catch outside with your kids,

1319
01:00:58.430 --> 01:01:00.000
during a couple of these meetings a week.

1320
01:01:00.000 --> 01:01:02.510
And I think that we've been too wedded

1321
01:01:02.510 --> 01:01:04.010
to the idea that you always have to sit

1322
01:01:04.010 --> 01:01:06.230
in front of a computer in order to be working,

1323
01:01:06.230 --> 01:01:07.720
and I just don't think that's true anymore,

1324
01:01:07.720 --> 01:01:10.230
and that's one of my basic list of things

1325
01:01:10.230 --> 01:01:12.380
we could probably rethink a little more than we do.

1326
01:01:12.380 --> 01:01:16.440
Yeah, I mean, I'm certainly no fan of how it's being used

1327
01:01:16.440 --> 01:01:20.410
in for high school education right now,

1328
01:01:20.410 --> 01:01:23.390
I've got a daughter who she's on Zoom

1329
01:01:23.390 --> 01:01:26.680
from like eight to four, doing classes,

1330
01:01:26.680 --> 01:01:28.480
and she's just not wired

1331
01:01:28.480 --> 01:01:30.610
to sit and stare at a screen like that.

1332
01:01:30.610 --> 01:01:31.780
And then she's got homework,

1333
01:01:31.780 --> 01:01:33.160
and she's in an art high school,

1334
01:01:33.160 --> 01:01:35.070
and then she's got her art projects.

1335
01:01:35.070 --> 01:01:37.890
And the stress level and the anxiety

1336
01:01:37.890 --> 01:01:39.670
with her is through the roof.

1337
01:01:39.670 --> 01:01:42.020
And I'm like, what is this for?

1338
01:01:42.020 --> 01:01:45.330
This is not in service to her best interest right now,

1339
01:01:45.330 --> 01:01:48.750
it's really deeply problematic I think.

1340
01:01:48.750 --> 01:01:52.240
I'm sad to hear that, and I'm also a little surprised,

1341
01:01:52.240 --> 01:01:54.750
because I've read evidence now,

1342
01:01:54.750 --> 01:01:57.090
both from a UK study and a US study,

1343
01:01:57.090 --> 01:02:00.110
that teenagers are actually doing better

1344
01:02:00.110 --> 01:02:02.190
from a well being perspective.

1345
01:02:02.190 --> 01:02:05.440
So lower depression, lower anxiety rates,

1346
01:02:05.440 --> 01:02:06.930
during parts of this pandemic.

1347
01:02:06.930 --> 01:02:10.040
And it seems to be the case that one of the big mechanisms

1348
01:02:10.040 --> 01:02:13.563
behind that is it's hard to get bullied over Zoom.

1349
01:02:14.600 --> 01:02:17.520
And you don't quite worry as much about what you're wearing,

1350
01:02:17.520 --> 01:02:20.250
and who's excluding you at the lunch table.

1351
01:02:20.250 --> 01:02:22.830
It's this virtual and I guess the virtual environment

1352
01:02:22.830 --> 01:02:24.930
is an equalizing force in some ways.

1353
01:02:24.930 --> 01:02:27.090
And it's almost just like at work

1354
01:02:27.090 --> 01:02:28.800
where it's this double edged sword,

1355
01:02:28.800 --> 01:02:29.830
there are things that got better,

1356
01:02:29.830 --> 01:02:31.300
there are things that are harder.

1357
01:02:31.300 --> 01:02:35.010
And I wonder if on average, that's the experience for,

1358
01:02:35.010 --> 01:02:37.080
at least to older kids in online school.

1359
01:02:37.080 --> 01:02:38.590
Yeah, I hadn't heard that before,

1360
01:02:38.590 --> 01:02:39.750
that's super interesting.

1361
01:02:39.750 --> 01:02:43.010
I mean, all the things that I'd seen were that depression

1362
01:02:43.010 --> 01:02:46.350
and anxiety were way higher, like on the rise

1363
01:02:46.350 --> 01:02:49.250
as a result of this with young people.

1364
01:02:49.250 --> 01:02:51.630
I think so it seems to be age specific.

1365
01:02:51.630 --> 01:02:53.740
I think younger kids are having a harder time,

1366
01:02:53.740 --> 01:02:57.250
I think for teenagers who are more tech savvy and have a lot

1367
01:02:57.250 --> 01:02:59.630
of the existing relationships where they can stay in touch,

1368
01:02:59.630 --> 01:03:03.146
and let's face it, a lot of them were just texting already.

1369
01:03:03.146 --> 01:03:05.230
So it's not like that everybody lost

1370
01:03:05.230 --> 01:03:07.060
all their in person time.

1371
01:03:07.060 --> 01:03:09.570
I think that piece of it seems to be different.

1372
01:03:09.570 --> 01:03:13.010
And then also, it's probably still too early

1373
01:03:13.010 --> 01:03:14.530
to have any real sense of this.

1374
01:03:14.530 --> 01:03:16.410
But my read from some of the initial data

1375
01:03:16.410 --> 01:03:19.320
is that during lockdown,

1376
01:03:19.320 --> 01:03:21.770
is when some of these well being benefits emerged,

1377
01:03:21.770 --> 01:03:24.880
where everybody was in the same remote scenario.

1378
01:03:24.880 --> 01:03:29.240
And yeah, I think, as we started to see more disparities

1379
01:03:29.240 --> 01:03:32.620
with some kids in school, some kids who are stuck remote,

1380
01:03:32.620 --> 01:03:36.930
I think that's probably exacerbated the problems

1381
01:03:36.930 --> 01:03:40.150
and maybe minimize some of the benefits to it.

1382
01:03:40.150 --> 01:03:43.840
Right, like there was a democratic, you know,

1383
01:03:43.840 --> 01:03:48.760
impact on everybody equally as a result of the lockdown.

1384
01:03:48.760 --> 01:03:51.630
Yeah, which is again, just like at work,

1385
01:03:51.630 --> 01:03:54.690
if 90% of your team is in the same place,

1386
01:03:54.690 --> 01:03:55.950
you don't wanna be the one person

1387
01:03:55.950 --> 01:03:58.213
calling in virtually, if you can avoid it.

1388
01:03:59.366 --> 01:04:02.260
From, you know, the Caribbean or something like that,

1389
01:04:02.260 --> 01:04:03.390
while everyone else is stuck in (indistinct).

1390
01:04:03.390 --> 01:04:04.910
I'm on the beach.
(Rich laughs)

1391
01:04:04.910 --> 01:04:05.997
Great to see you all.

1392
01:04:05.997 --> 01:04:07.470
Great, well, I want to get back

1393
01:04:07.470 --> 01:04:11.250
to some of the ideas in "Think Again."

1394
01:04:11.250 --> 01:04:13.890
And we were talking about Michigan earlier,

1395
01:04:13.890 --> 01:04:17.760
I mean, when I was young, we moved to Washington, DC,

1396
01:04:17.760 --> 01:04:19.960
which is really where I grew up.

1397
01:04:19.960 --> 01:04:22.040
And my dad was a government lawyer.

1398
01:04:22.040 --> 01:04:27.040
So we were kind of in this inside the Beltway community.

1399
01:04:27.080 --> 01:04:31.320
And this is like, you know, the late 70s, early 80s.

1400
01:04:31.320 --> 01:04:35.620
And, you know, in our neighborhood, there were Republicans,

1401
01:04:35.620 --> 01:04:39.930
Democrats, my next door neighbor was a was a senator like,

1402
01:04:39.930 --> 01:04:43.410
and I just recall, even though there were all kinds

1403
01:04:43.410 --> 01:04:46.820
of people on different sides of the aisle politically,

1404
01:04:46.820 --> 01:04:51.660
there was a level of comedy that I think has disappeared.

1405
01:04:51.660 --> 01:04:54.380
Like we were friends with all these people,

1406
01:04:54.380 --> 01:04:57.370
we could have parties and they would all come over

1407
01:04:57.370 --> 01:04:59.660
and everybody got along just fine,

1408
01:04:59.660 --> 01:05:02.610
and this is really no longer the case.

1409
01:05:02.610 --> 01:05:06.940
And I do find myself despairing over our ability

1410
01:05:06.940 --> 01:05:11.310
to commune productively and communicate effectively,

1411
01:05:11.310 --> 01:05:14.260
and your book is very much an antidote to that.

1412
01:05:14.260 --> 01:05:17.413
But whatever, you know, the Gestalt of social forces

1413
01:05:17.413 --> 01:05:21.020
have put us into this place, like, how is this?

1414
01:05:21.020 --> 01:05:23.000
You know, how does this play out for you?

1415
01:05:23.000 --> 01:05:24.710
Are we gonna continue on this path?

1416
01:05:24.710 --> 01:05:29.240
Like I know, you're looking at this mostly

1417
01:05:29.240 --> 01:05:32.200
from the perspective of what we can do as individuals

1418
01:05:32.200 --> 01:05:36.630
and organizations to rebut this, but on a more kind of,

1419
01:05:36.630 --> 01:05:39.810
national scale, like, how are you thinking

1420
01:05:39.810 --> 01:05:44.003
about the future of our ability to communicate healthily?

1421
01:05:45.330 --> 01:05:46.820
I've been thinking about it a lot.

1422
01:05:46.820 --> 01:05:49.050
And my first thought is always,

1423
01:05:49.050 --> 01:05:50.960
as an organizational psychologist, I don't have

1424
01:05:50.960 --> 01:05:55.160
the expertise to even begin to analyze this question.

1425
01:05:55.160 --> 01:05:56.980
We should be talking to political scientists

1426
01:05:56.980 --> 01:05:59.710
and sociologists, probably some anthropologists as well,

1427
01:05:59.710 --> 01:06:02.260
because I think we're essentially in a place now

1428
01:06:02.260 --> 01:06:06.360
where we've got two foreign cultures that do not like

1429
01:06:06.360 --> 01:06:09.760
what the other side is supposedly standing for.

1430
01:06:09.760 --> 01:06:13.420
And so most of what I've been doing is just a lot of reading

1431
01:06:13.420 --> 01:06:16.090
and a lot of asking questions to try to understand

1432
01:06:16.090 --> 01:06:18.623
the causes and some of the remedies better.

1433
01:06:19.920 --> 01:06:21.530
I don't know where we go from here.

1434
01:06:21.530 --> 01:06:24.860
I think that one of the things that happen,

1435
01:06:24.860 --> 01:06:29.300
post election, post insurrection is, I just saw people,

1436
01:06:29.300 --> 01:06:33.810
I really respect intellectually, get crushed.

1437
01:06:35.160 --> 01:06:37.500
And in some cases, people attempted

1438
01:06:37.500 --> 01:06:40.233
to cancel them for calling for respect.

1439
01:06:41.520 --> 01:06:45.000
And I don't see how we make any progress,

1440
01:06:45.000 --> 01:06:49.130
without giving people just the basic dignity they're owed,

1441
01:06:49.130 --> 01:06:50.163
as human beings.

1442
01:06:51.090 --> 01:06:53.360
I like this distinction that Christy Rogers

1443
01:06:53.360 --> 01:06:55.550
and her colleagues make, which is between owed

1444
01:06:55.550 --> 01:06:58.610
and earned respect, owed respect being that basic,

1445
01:06:58.610 --> 01:07:00.480
you are a human and therefore,

1446
01:07:00.480 --> 01:07:04.630
I think your ideas and your emotions matter.

1447
01:07:04.630 --> 01:07:06.890
And then earned respect being okay,

1448
01:07:06.890 --> 01:07:09.520
depending on your performance or your contribution,

1449
01:07:09.520 --> 01:07:13.740
or your credibility, I might listen to you more carefully,

1450
01:07:13.740 --> 01:07:14.950
I might take what you say

1451
01:07:14.950 --> 01:07:17.770
a little bit more seriously in my own life.

1452
01:07:17.770 --> 01:07:21.380
And I think the idea that we should abandon owed respect,

1453
01:07:21.380 --> 01:07:24.617
that you can solve this problem by saying,

1454
01:07:24.617 --> 01:07:26.510
"Well, I think you don't respect me,

1455
01:07:26.510 --> 01:07:28.736
and therefore I'm not gonna respect you."

1456
01:07:28.736 --> 01:07:32.510
Like that's first grade playground behavior.

1457
01:07:32.510 --> 01:07:34.680
I think that we need to be clear

1458
01:07:34.680 --> 01:07:38.030
about how we earn respect in this society.

1459
01:07:38.030 --> 01:07:41.020
And ideally, I mean, this is extreme, but I would love

1460
01:07:41.020 --> 01:07:43.660
to see people start keeping score a little bit.

1461
01:07:43.660 --> 01:07:46.270
Let's go through, you know, we have a lot of debate

1462
01:07:46.270 --> 01:07:49.470
about media as media and social media.

1463
01:07:49.470 --> 01:07:53.400
Let's go through people who make claims and predictions.

1464
01:07:53.400 --> 01:07:54.790
And let's actually track

1465
01:07:54.790 --> 01:07:56.780
who turns out to be right and wrong.

1466
01:07:56.780 --> 01:07:58.750
And let's hold ourselves accountable

1467
01:07:58.750 --> 01:08:01.690
before we have the data, for following and listening

1468
01:08:01.690 --> 01:08:04.370
to the people who get things right more often.

1469
01:08:04.370 --> 01:08:06.630
And Rich' as you know, my read of the evidence

1470
01:08:06.630 --> 01:08:08.850
is that people who get things right more often,

1471
01:08:08.850 --> 01:08:11.350
are the people who admit they're wrong more often.

1472
01:08:12.514 --> 01:08:15.290
And that needs to become a currency socially.

1473
01:08:15.290 --> 01:08:17.620
Saying you're wrong needs to be a sign

1474
01:08:17.620 --> 01:08:19.780
that you are willing to think like a scientist,

1475
01:08:19.780 --> 01:08:21.320
that you are interested in learning

1476
01:08:21.320 --> 01:08:22.960
and updating your beliefs,

1477
01:08:22.960 --> 01:08:25.290
when better logic or data come along.

1478
01:08:25.290 --> 01:08:26.640
And I don't know how to get us there,

1479
01:08:26.640 --> 01:08:28.240
but that's where I wanna go.

1480
01:08:28.240 --> 01:08:30.330
Well, the keeping score thing though,

1481
01:08:30.330 --> 01:08:32.310
is part of the problem as well,

1482
01:08:32.310 --> 01:08:33.680
like when you look at Twitter,

1483
01:08:33.680 --> 01:08:36.230
it's all about, it's not about truth or nuance,

1484
01:08:36.230 --> 01:08:38.860
it's about signaling to your respective tribe,

1485
01:08:38.860 --> 01:08:40.810
that you're a member in good standing.

1486
01:08:40.810 --> 01:08:43.700
And the point scoring is all about taking people down

1487
01:08:43.700 --> 01:08:46.860
or holding people to account for an idea

1488
01:08:46.860 --> 01:08:49.980
that doesn't neatly fit into the, you know,

1489
01:08:49.980 --> 01:08:54.980
the prescribed ideology of that particular tribe.

1490
01:08:56.430 --> 01:08:58.660
I don't think it has to be that way, though, right?

1491
01:08:58.660 --> 01:09:01.400
There are communities on Twitter that have adopted

1492
01:09:01.400 --> 01:09:03.460
different values and different norms.

1493
01:09:03.460 --> 01:09:06.300
I know academic Twitter is a popular hashtag,

1494
01:09:06.300 --> 01:09:09.380
and there are entire sub-fields of people.

1495
01:09:09.380 --> 01:09:11.490
We started with epidemiologists last spring

1496
01:09:11.490 --> 01:09:13.810
and continuing to the present.

1497
01:09:13.810 --> 01:09:16.030
We've seen it with people who are debating

1498
01:09:16.030 --> 01:09:21.030
hot issues across very complex fields, I think.

1499
01:09:22.360 --> 01:09:25.110
I mean, I've made a concerted effort to follow people

1500
01:09:25.110 --> 01:09:27.100
whose conclusions I disagree with,

1501
01:09:27.100 --> 01:09:29.580
because I admire the integrity of their thought process.

1502
01:09:29.580 --> 01:09:31.760
And that means I see information

1503
01:09:31.760 --> 01:09:36.580
that constantly questions my convictions.

1504
01:09:36.580 --> 01:09:37.830
But that's not a threat to me,

1505
01:09:37.830 --> 01:09:39.430
I don't have ego invested in that,

1506
01:09:39.430 --> 01:09:41.820
I'm on Twitter to learn and to teach.

1507
01:09:41.820 --> 01:09:46.820
And that means if I'm accessing different perspectives

1508
01:09:47.670 --> 01:09:50.640
from people who have high standards of rigor, I'm learning,

1509
01:09:50.640 --> 01:09:54.970
and I think your point is well taken, that social media,

1510
01:09:54.970 --> 01:09:56.510
and I think the traditional media has done this

1511
01:09:56.510 --> 01:10:01.510
for a long time to really rewards preaching and prosecuting.

1512
01:10:01.530 --> 01:10:05.180
Like the person with a sound bite that can kinda elevate

1513
01:10:05.180 --> 01:10:07.570
their tribe and take down the other tribe,

1514
