01:21:43.700 --> 01:21:45.410
why I asked about diving at the outset.

1761
01:21:45.410 --> 01:21:49.210
Like, you have this ability to execute on your goals,

1762
01:21:49.210 --> 01:21:52.310
like you got a masters and a PhD in three years

1763
01:21:52.310 --> 01:21:53.910
at University of Michigan.

1764
01:21:53.910 --> 01:21:56.790
There's this story that you always email people back

1765
01:21:56.790 --> 01:21:58.500
right away, I've experienced that,

1766
01:21:58.500 --> 01:21:59.520
like, I don't know how you do it,

1767
01:21:59.520 --> 01:22:01.280
like, there are certain aspects,

1768
01:22:01.280 --> 01:22:04.770
like character specific aspects of who you are,

1769
01:22:04.770 --> 01:22:07.000
that you seem to have developed mastery over

1770
01:22:07.000 --> 01:22:08.680
and whether you learn those as a diver

1771
01:22:08.680 --> 01:22:11.950
or as a diligent student, I don't know.

1772
01:22:11.950 --> 01:22:16.750
But how can you speak to the person who suffers

1773
01:22:16.750 --> 01:22:20.730
from an inability to marry their value systems

1774
01:22:20.730 --> 01:22:24.160
with their output or their actions, does that make sense?

1775
01:22:24.160 --> 01:22:27.720
It does, it's honestly something I've had a hard time

1776
01:22:27.720 --> 01:22:30.353
making sense of, because it's just so foreign to me.

1777
01:22:31.348 --> 01:22:32.181
(indistinct)

1778
01:22:32.181 --> 01:22:33.610
It's hardwired into you, right?

1779
01:22:33.610 --> 01:22:36.990
Yeah, I mean, I don't even think, I don't think

1780
01:22:36.990 --> 01:22:39.840
I learned any of the tendencies you described, right?

1781
01:22:39.840 --> 01:22:41.940
I think they're just built in, I remember,

1782
01:22:43.819 --> 01:22:45.840
if you had a time machine

1783
01:22:45.840 --> 01:22:48.470
and you went to meet five year old me,

1784
01:22:48.470 --> 01:22:50.980
I had a goal of waking up as early as possible

1785
01:22:50.980 --> 01:22:52.610
in the morning so that I could play

1786
01:22:52.610 --> 01:22:54.768
with human characters.

1787
01:22:54.768 --> 01:22:56.340
I had a whole story that I wanted to play out,

1788
01:22:56.340 --> 01:22:58.610
nothing was interfering with that.

1789
01:22:58.610 --> 01:23:00.420
The Nintendo thing too, right?

1790
01:23:00.420 --> 01:23:03.900
Same, yeah, Nintendo is just laser focused obsession.

1791
01:23:03.900 --> 01:23:05.710
And my problem has always been,

1792
01:23:05.710 --> 01:23:08.020
how do I lower those attentional filters

1793
01:23:08.020 --> 01:23:09.440
so that they're not blinders?

1794
01:23:09.440 --> 01:23:14.150
And it's, you know, it's almost like when I meet somebody

1795
01:23:14.150 --> 01:23:15.290
who has the opposite challenge,

1796
01:23:15.290 --> 01:23:17.300
which I realize is more common than mine.

1797
01:23:17.300 --> 01:23:19.900
Like, I'm really, I have no idea

1798
01:23:19.900 --> 01:23:21.940
because I don't do this deliberately,

1799
01:23:21.940 --> 01:23:23.193
it's just how I'm wired.

1800
01:23:24.320 --> 01:23:26.960
I think, I mean, the best thing that I've learned

1801
01:23:26.960 --> 01:23:31.010
from trying to study it, is really what I ended up exploring

1802
01:23:31.010 --> 01:23:33.440
on procrastination last year.

1803
01:23:33.440 --> 01:23:36.120
So I give a whole TED Talk

1804
01:23:36.120 --> 01:23:38.140
that's about the surprising virtues

1805
01:23:38.140 --> 01:23:40.060
of moderate procrastination for creativity,

1806
01:23:40.060 --> 01:23:42.030
I read about that in a book.

1807
01:23:42.030 --> 01:23:45.050
And then I find that most people

1808
01:23:45.050 --> 01:23:47.170
are not precrastinators like me,

1809
01:23:47.170 --> 01:23:50.190
they're really struggling with procrastination

1810
01:23:50.190 --> 01:23:52.300
that's hurting their productivity,

1811
01:23:52.300 --> 01:23:54.860
that's undermining their romantic relationships,

1812
01:23:54.860 --> 01:23:57.640
that's causing them to be unhealthy.

1813
01:23:57.640 --> 01:24:00.470
And I went and said, "Okay, I've got to understand

1814
01:24:00.470 --> 01:24:01.720
what do we know about the psychology

1815
01:24:01.720 --> 01:24:02.950
of preventing procrastination?"

1816
01:24:02.950 --> 01:24:04.640
And the light bulb moment

1817
01:24:04.640 --> 01:24:08.570
for me was some work by Fuchsia Sora and Tim Petula,

1818
01:24:08.570 --> 01:24:12.310
two psychologists who have found that when we procrastinate,

1819
01:24:12.310 --> 01:24:15.660
it's not because of being, it's not because of laziness.

1820
01:24:15.660 --> 01:24:16.760
And I think you could probably say

1821
01:24:16.760 --> 01:24:18.000
this is more broadly true, right?

1822
01:24:18.000 --> 01:24:20.930
It's not procrastination, many of the gaps

1823
01:24:20.930 --> 01:24:24.760
between people's goals and values and their daily actions,

1824
01:24:24.760 --> 01:24:27.530
they're not because they don't wanna work hard,

1825
01:24:27.530 --> 01:24:32.190
or they lack you know, some magical amount of grid,

1826
01:24:32.190 --> 01:24:35.490
that's a prerequisite for following through.

1827
01:24:35.490 --> 01:24:39.683
It's often because of emotions, not time.

1828
01:24:40.900 --> 01:24:43.110
And what they're avoiding is not hard work,

1829
01:24:43.110 --> 01:24:45.010
they're avoiding a negative emotion

1830
01:24:45.010 --> 01:24:47.740
that a particular task stirs up.

1831
01:24:47.740 --> 01:24:49.330
So, what I would encourage people to do is to think

1832
01:24:49.330 --> 01:24:51.470
about whatever goal that you're not pursuing,

1833
01:24:51.470 --> 01:24:54.227
or that you're, you know, whatever habit you're trying

1834
01:24:54.227 --> 01:24:55.720
to build that you're falling short on,

1835
01:24:55.720 --> 01:24:59.160
and ask yourself what negative emotion is associated,

1836
01:24:59.160 --> 01:25:01.090
with the behavior that you wanna do more of,

1837
01:25:01.090 --> 01:25:04.400
or the behavior that you wanna stop doing?

1838
01:25:04.400 --> 01:25:06.900
And you know, in some cases, it's fear for people, right?

1839
01:25:06.900 --> 01:25:10.690
They'll say, I'm afraid of finding out that I can't do it,

1840
01:25:10.690 --> 01:25:14.170
and I would rather just put in partial effort

1841
01:25:14.170 --> 01:25:15.480
and sandbag it.

1842
01:25:15.480 --> 01:25:18.070
And by self handicapping,

1843
01:25:18.070 --> 01:25:20.630
I don't have to ever find out if I'm capable or not.

1844
01:25:20.630 --> 01:25:22.120
And that way I can believe

1845
01:25:22.120 --> 01:25:24.440
that I really have the potential one day.

1846
01:25:24.440 --> 01:25:28.380
For other people it's frustration,

1847
01:25:28.380 --> 01:25:30.310
it's I tried a bunch of times,

1848
01:25:30.310 --> 01:25:31.930
I didn't get the result I wanted,

1849
01:25:31.930 --> 01:25:34.300
and I'm tired of banging my head against a brick wall.

1850
01:25:34.300 --> 01:25:38.050
And so it's just, it's not worth the effort anymore.

1851
01:25:38.050 --> 01:25:39.130
For me, it's boredom.

1852
01:25:39.130 --> 01:25:41.140
It turns out, I procrastinate sometimes Rich,

1853
01:25:41.140 --> 01:25:43.170
and when I procrastinate because--

1854
01:25:43.170 --> 01:25:44.320
Shame on you.

1855
01:25:44.320 --> 01:25:48.060
I know, I feel I feel like such a fraud, I'm a hypocrite.

1856
01:25:48.060 --> 01:25:50.033
But everyone procrastinate sometimes, right?

1857
01:25:50.033 --> 01:25:51.930
It's a normal human behavior.

1858
01:25:51.930 --> 01:25:55.610
And when I do it, it's because what fires me up

1859
01:25:55.610 --> 01:25:58.700
is intrinsic motivation, the intrinsic motivation

1860
01:25:58.700 --> 01:26:02.010
to work on an interesting problem, to help somebody.

1861
01:26:02.010 --> 01:26:05.370
And if I have to do something that I think is not exciting,

1862
01:26:05.370 --> 01:26:07.810
and that doesn't directly benefit people,

1863
