2251
01:43:31.690 --> 01:43:34.100
in fact that violates our value of freedom.

2252
01:43:34.100 --> 01:43:36.090
Right, you can't threaten someone else's freedom,

2253
01:43:36.090 --> 01:43:38.190
then you're not in a free country anymore.

2254
01:43:39.040 --> 01:43:40.410
So I have a hard time with that.

2255
01:43:40.410 --> 01:43:42.430
And I also have a hard time with,

2256
01:43:42.430 --> 01:43:45.150
when I am just dead set on the fact

2257
01:43:45.150 --> 01:43:47.810
that the way that they're trying to live their values,

2258
01:43:47.810 --> 01:43:49.900
is not gonna work out well for them

2259
01:43:49.900 --> 01:43:52.200
or for the people that they care about.

2260
01:43:52.200 --> 01:43:53.170
And those are the moments

2261
01:43:53.170 --> 01:43:56.270
where I get stuck going back into prosecutor mode,

2262
01:43:56.270 --> 01:43:58.490
but I think the rest of the time,

2263
01:43:58.490 --> 01:44:01.420
where I think I've had some luck.

2264
01:44:01.420 --> 01:44:05.020
And some, I guess, made some progress,

2265
01:44:05.020 --> 01:44:07.150
has been when I've said, you know what Rich?

2266
01:44:07.150 --> 01:44:09.780
Let me just start the conversation by recognizing

2267
01:44:09.780 --> 01:44:13.040
that I have a bad habit of trying to change your mind,

2268
01:44:13.040 --> 01:44:14.760
and I'm trying to get out of that habit.

2269
01:44:14.760 --> 01:44:16.950
And so what I really wanna do here is interview you,

2270
01:44:16.950 --> 01:44:17.880
I wanna learn from you,

2271
01:44:17.880 --> 01:44:20.010
I wanna understand your viewpoint better.

2272
01:44:20.010 --> 01:44:23.620
And I do have a vested interest in people having open minds,

2273
01:44:23.620 --> 01:44:26.250
because I believe that that's foundational for learning.

2274
01:44:26.250 --> 01:44:27.920
And so you might catch my questions

2275
01:44:27.920 --> 01:44:30.210
being a little bit tilted in the direction of,

2276
01:44:30.210 --> 01:44:32.010
you know, are there are there circumstances

2277
01:44:32.010 --> 01:44:33.430
that would shift your opinion a little bit?

2278
01:44:33.430 --> 01:44:35.040
And that's not because I'm trying

2279
01:44:35.040 --> 01:44:36.850
to drive you in a particular direction,

2280
01:44:36.850 --> 01:44:39.380
it's because I'm invested in a personal project,

2281
01:44:39.380 --> 01:44:42.330
of building a more mentally flexible world right now.

2282
01:44:42.330 --> 01:44:44.790
And if you object to that project, I wanna hear that too,

2283
01:44:44.790 --> 01:44:46.840
because I need to be flexible.

2284
01:44:46.840 --> 01:44:50.540
And when I've started with that kind of disclaimer upfront,

2285
01:44:50.540 --> 01:44:52.140
it's I think, both sent the right message

2286
01:44:52.140 --> 01:44:54.530
to the other person, but it's also reinforced,

2287
01:44:54.530 --> 01:44:56.720
like that's me activating cognitive dissonance

2288
01:44:56.720 --> 01:44:57.740
if I screw up,

2289
01:44:57.740 --> 01:45:00.340
and then I'm more likely to hold myself accountable.

2290
01:45:00.340 --> 01:45:05.340
It is interesting, how reluctant we are to wanna be wrong.

2291
01:45:05.820 --> 01:45:08.617
Like, it shouldn't be such a big deal to say,

2292
01:45:08.617 --> 01:45:11.500
"I don't know," or like, "Wow, I was wrong about that,"

2293
01:45:11.500 --> 01:45:13.850
like to be able to pivot,

2294
01:45:13.850 --> 01:45:16.480
I mean, certainly, you know, in the public sphere,

2295
01:45:16.480 --> 01:45:19.730
or if you're a politician, it's seen as anathema

2296
01:45:19.730 --> 01:45:21.640
for you to change your mind.

2297
01:45:21.640 --> 01:45:25.320
You know, it does feel like in order to cultivate

2298
01:45:25.320 --> 01:45:30.320
a healthy culture, we need to incentivize open mindedness,

2299
01:45:31.510 --> 01:45:36.510
on a broader scale, and de-stigmatize this idea

2300
01:45:37.000 --> 01:45:41.470
of not knowing and of saying, I'm wrong, it's okay, right?

2301
01:45:41.470 --> 01:45:44.960
Like our ego attachment to that is so powerful.

2302
01:45:44.960 --> 01:45:47.130
Yeah, I think it is.

2303
01:45:47.130 --> 01:45:49.993
And I don't know how to do that societally.

2304
01:45:51.260 --> 01:45:53.750
I've tried to figure out what can we do, you know,

2305
01:45:53.750 --> 01:45:56.537
in schools and workplaces, even in families, right?

2306
01:45:56.537 --> 01:45:58.250
And in cultures or systems that we have

2307
01:45:58.250 --> 01:45:59.780
a little bit more direct influence over.

2308
01:45:59.780 --> 01:46:02.530
And I think the news there is actually pretty good, right?

2309
01:46:02.530 --> 01:46:04.700
That when students are taught,

2310
01:46:04.700 --> 01:46:06.590
that admitting what they don't know,

2311
01:46:06.590 --> 01:46:08.660
is actually part of the learning process,

2312
01:46:08.660 --> 01:46:10.770
they become more curious than intellectually humble,

2313
01:46:10.770 --> 01:46:12.870
they actually learn more and do better on tests.

2314
01:46:12.870 --> 01:46:14.830
And so creating that kind of psychological safety

2315
01:46:14.830 --> 01:46:16.357
in your classroom, for anyone to say,

2316
01:46:16.357 --> 01:46:17.680
"Hey, I didn't understand that."

2317
01:46:17.680 --> 01:46:20.090
And that's not a sign that I lack intelligence.

2318
01:46:20.090 --> 01:46:22.930
It's a sign that I wanna pick up knowledge.

2319
01:46:22.930 --> 01:46:24.630
That seems to be a healthy attitude

2320
01:46:24.630 --> 01:46:26.380
for a classroom environment.

2321
01:46:26.380 --> 01:46:29.188
Workplace, it's the same thing, right?

2322
01:46:29.188 --> 01:46:32.433
Let's stop making expertise the currency of success.

2323
01:46:33.640 --> 01:46:37.040
Let's say what we wanna do is recognize

2324
01:46:37.040 --> 01:46:39.720
that knowledge is not a weapon to wield,

2325
01:46:39.720 --> 01:46:41.290
it's a resource to share.

2326
01:46:41.290 --> 01:46:43.420
And that means if you lack knowledge,

2327
01:46:43.420 --> 01:46:45.060
you should not only be given permission,

2328
01:46:45.060 --> 01:46:47.360
you should be encouraged to go to people

2329
01:46:47.360 --> 01:46:49.600
who might have a perspective that you lack,

2330
01:46:49.600 --> 01:46:51.100
and let them know what your ignorance is,

2331
01:46:51.100 --> 01:46:53.040
so that you can try to fill some of the gaps

2332
01:46:53.040 --> 01:46:54.280
in your knowledge.

2333
01:46:54.280 --> 01:46:58.240
And I think that starts with leaders not punishing people,

2334
01:46:58.240 --> 01:47:01.460
for asking questions or for being wrong.

2335
01:47:01.460 --> 01:47:03.830
It starts with, in many cases,

2336
01:47:03.830 --> 01:47:06.630
just doing something really basic, which is,

2337
01:47:06.630 --> 01:47:09.500
don't just reward results,

2338
01:47:09.500 --> 01:47:12.650
don't just reward success and punish failure.

2339
01:47:12.650 --> 01:47:15.960
Put people in a situation where you reward a thoughtful,

2340
01:47:15.960 --> 01:47:17.940
curious, decision making process.

2341
01:47:17.940 --> 01:47:20.550
And that means we could even celebrate

2342
01:47:21.800 --> 01:47:24.060
a good decision process with a bad outcome.

2343
01:47:24.060 --> 01:47:26.220
Because maybe that was a smart experiment to run,

2344
01:47:26.220 --> 01:47:27.940
and we learned something from it.

2345
01:47:27.940 --> 01:47:30.840
And maybe we should stop rewarding good outcomes

2346
01:47:30.840 --> 01:47:33.300
with bad processes, because that's just luck.

2347
01:47:33.300 --> 01:47:35.040
And you don't wanna repeat that,

2348
01:47:35.040 --> 01:47:37.340
you wanna rethink your process.

2349
01:47:37.340 --> 01:47:39.900
But the idea being that this is trainable,

2350
01:47:39.900 --> 01:47:41.780
we are malleable in this regard.

2351
01:47:41.780 --> 01:47:45.220
We're not born as somebody who loves to be proved wrong,

2352
01:47:45.220 --> 01:47:47.220
like we can create incentives around this

2353
01:47:47.220 --> 01:47:50.540
in our organizations and in our interpersonal lives,

2354
01:47:50.540 --> 01:47:53.060
to cultivate a little bit more of this,

2355
01:47:53.060 --> 01:47:55.410
and to the extent that we can do that,

2356
01:47:55.410 --> 01:47:58.780
we become, we stress testing our own ideas,

2357
01:47:58.780 --> 01:48:01.140
we become better, more creative thinkers,

2358
01:48:01.140 --> 01:48:04.203
we become better conversationalist, we become better,

2359
01:48:05.200 --> 01:48:08.290
challenge network members, for our friends and our peers,

2360
01:48:08.290 --> 01:48:12.230
and ultimately, just be better humans and citizens,

2361
01:48:12.230 --> 01:48:14.520
is that fair?
(both laugh)

2362
01:48:14.520 --> 01:48:17.610
It's more than fair, that's a hell of an elevator pitch

2363
01:48:17.610 --> 01:48:21.520
for what I hope people take away from this set of ideas.

2364
01:48:21.520 --> 01:48:25.210
And yeah, I don't think any of that resistance

2365
01:48:25.210 --> 01:48:26.490
is hardwired, right?

2366
01:48:26.490 --> 01:48:28.630
There's no reason why you couldn't create,

2367
01:48:28.630 --> 01:48:31.460
a culture or a community where people

2368
01:48:31.460 --> 01:48:33.810
are punished if they don't admit they're wrong.

2369
01:48:34.760 --> 01:48:38.010
And I think we just gotten that backward

2370
01:48:38.010 --> 01:48:40.050
in a lot of parts of the world.

2371
01:48:40.050 --> 01:48:42.090
And, you know, I don't know about you,

2372
01:48:42.090 --> 01:48:44.790
but I would rather reward people

2373
01:48:44.790 --> 01:48:47.240
who are interested in developing competence,

2374
01:48:47.240 --> 01:48:49.890
than hold up people who are full

2375
01:48:49.890 --> 01:48:52.610
of overconfidence as exemplars to follow.

2376
01:48:52.610 --> 01:48:54.530
Yeah, you and me both my friend?

2377
01:48:54.530 --> 01:48:58.430
Well, I think that's a great place to end it.

2378
01:48:58.430 --> 01:49:01.390
But before we land, the plane, I think it would be helpful

2379
01:49:01.390 --> 01:49:06.023
or instructive for the listener or the viewer to have,

2380
01:49:06.910 --> 01:49:09.120
I think the idea that I wanna leave people with is,

2381
01:49:09.120 --> 01:49:13.910
if you feel yourself resistant to a different idea,

2382
01:49:13.910 --> 01:49:17.570
or there's a certain personality out there that really,

2383
01:49:17.570 --> 01:49:19.330
gets your blood boiling.

2384
01:49:19.330 --> 01:49:24.020
Like what's the first step for that person to take,

2385
01:49:24.020 --> 01:49:26.900
in terms of trying to soften a little bit

2386
01:49:26.900 --> 01:49:28.650
so that perhaps they could have

2387
01:49:28.650 --> 01:49:30.423
a more productive interaction?

2388
01:49:31.850 --> 01:49:34.610
I think what I would recommend is to treat

2389
01:49:34.610 --> 01:49:37.363
that emotional response as a rough draft.

2390
01:49:38.426 --> 01:49:42.420
It's the first draft of how you're gonna feel.

2391
01:49:42.420 --> 01:49:43.900
And I've never done anything

2392
01:49:43.900 --> 01:49:46.090
where my first draft was any good.

2393
01:49:46.090 --> 01:49:49.280
Where I didn't wanna constantly revise and improve it.

2394
01:49:49.280 --> 01:49:51.050
Whether it was the first time I tried to dive

2395
01:49:51.050 --> 01:49:55.090
or the first draft of a book that I had to throw out,

2396
01:49:55.090 --> 01:49:58.040
or the TED Talk that I rewrote from scratch

2397
01:49:58.040 --> 01:49:59.500
over and over and over again,

2398
01:49:59.500 --> 01:50:01.870
and then everybody who achieves excellence in any domain

2399
01:50:01.870 --> 01:50:04.510
knows that it's the constant rewriting and rethinking,

2400
01:50:04.510 --> 01:50:08.170
and revising that makes you good and helps you get better.

2401
01:50:08.170 --> 01:50:11.160
And I think once you recognize, hey, you know what?

2402
01:50:11.160 --> 01:50:14.020
Feeling threatened or hating somebody else,

2403
01:50:14.020 --> 01:50:15.960
or being offended by somebody else,

2404
01:50:15.960 --> 01:50:18.310
that's a first draft of a response,

2405
01:50:18.310 --> 01:50:21.027
then you can go and write a revision, and ask yourself,

2406
01:50:21.027 --> 01:50:23.200
"Okay, is that a teachable moment?

2407
01:50:23.200 --> 01:50:24.450
Did I just learn something

2408
01:50:24.450 --> 01:50:28.110
about what activates my prosecutor instincts?

2409
01:50:28.110 --> 01:50:30.300
Or what puts me in a preaching mindset?"

2410
01:50:30.300 --> 01:50:31.883
And if I understand that better,

2411
01:50:31.883 --> 01:50:34.883
then I have more control over what mindset I landed?

2412
01:50:35.730 --> 01:50:39.270
Beautifully put, I think you practiced that

2413
01:50:39.270 --> 01:50:42.340
in the conclusion of your book, where you basically,

2414
01:50:42.340 --> 01:50:45.140
in this very really funny and like meta way,

2415
01:50:45.140 --> 01:50:47.940
like you basically, your conclusion is a red line.

2416
01:50:47.940 --> 01:50:51.210
And that shows like the changes and the thoughts that went

2417
01:50:51.210 --> 01:50:55.210
into various drafts of how you wanted to conclude this book,

2418
01:50:55.210 --> 01:50:57.160
which is not about conclusions,

2419
01:50:57.160 --> 01:51:01.650
but about remaining open to new ideas and possibilities.

2420
01:51:01.650 --> 01:51:03.395
I'm so glad you got a kick out of that,

2421
01:51:03.395 --> 01:51:04.228
because it was one of the things

2422
01:51:04.228 --> 01:51:07.330
that was most resistant to rethinking when I wrote the book.

2423
01:51:07.330 --> 01:51:10.130
I had argument after argument with this challenge network

2424
01:51:10.130 --> 01:51:12.800
of brilliant former students who read early drafts,

2425
01:51:12.800 --> 01:51:17.610
and I said, nonfiction book does not need a conclusion.

2426
01:51:17.610 --> 01:51:19.090
Like it's not a book report.

2427
01:51:19.090 --> 01:51:22.800
And anything else I had to say I would have already said it.

2428
01:51:22.800 --> 01:51:25.890
And they just, they were so thoughtful

2429
01:51:25.890 --> 01:51:28.570
and convincing me that, "Yeah, you know what?

2430
01:51:28.570 --> 01:51:31.430
I get that you're writing a book about rethinking,

2431
01:51:31.430 --> 01:51:33.610
you don't want the rethinking to end,

2432
01:51:33.610 --> 01:51:36.060
but the last page of this book is the beginning

2433
01:51:36.060 --> 01:51:37.750
of conversations we're all gonna have.

2434
01:51:37.750 --> 01:51:39.630
And it's the start of a reflection process.

2435
01:51:39.630 --> 01:51:41.944
And don't you wanna give us a springboard to that?"

2436
01:51:41.944 --> 01:51:42.777
(Adam roars)

2437
01:51:42.777 --> 01:51:44.890
All right, fine, maybe you're onto something here.

2438
01:51:44.890 --> 01:51:47.627
But very begrudgingly, and then I said,

2439
01:51:47.627 --> 01:51:49.330
"Well, yeah, you know how I could do that?

2440
01:51:49.330 --> 01:51:50.530
a blank page.

2441
01:51:50.530 --> 01:51:52.220
I'm just, you're gonna turn to the epilogue,

2442
01:51:52.220 --> 01:51:53.510
and there's gonna be nothing there,

2443
01:51:53.510 --> 01:51:55.670
and it's gonna be the the canvas for you

2444
01:51:55.670 --> 01:51:57.370
to do your rethinking on."

2445
01:51:57.370 --> 01:51:58.730
And they said, "Yeah, that's a cop out,

2446
01:51:58.730 --> 01:51:59.960
we can't let you do it.

2447
01:51:59.960 --> 01:52:01.990
Why don't you show us your own rethinking?"

2448
01:52:01.990 --> 01:52:05.660
I was like, "All right, I get that this would be very meta,

2449
01:52:05.660 --> 01:52:07.290
and I could have some fun with it.

2450
01:52:07.290 --> 01:52:10.017
But I really just wanna be done, I like closure."

2451
01:52:11.369 --> 01:52:13.117
I'm not living the principles I wrote about in the book,

2452
01:52:13.117 --> 01:52:14.690
I need to rethink this.

2453
01:52:14.690 --> 01:52:16.780
Right, well I thought it was great,

2454
01:52:16.780 --> 01:52:18.760
I'd never seen anything like that in a book.

2455
01:52:18.760 --> 01:52:21.960
And it's the perfect book to end in that manner,

2456
01:52:21.960 --> 01:52:24.530
in this kind of circular way that leaves it open ended,

2457
01:52:24.530 --> 01:52:27.180
and yet still create some finality for the reader.

2458
01:52:27.180 --> 01:52:30.960
So well done, I love the book,

2459
01:52:30.960 --> 01:52:34.700
I so appreciate the thoughtfulness

2460
01:52:34.700 --> 01:52:38.740
that you put into your work, the care, you are a true giver.

2461
01:52:38.740 --> 01:52:41.420
And it's been great to spend some time with you.

2462
01:52:41.420 --> 01:52:43.070
So thank you.

2463
01:52:43.070 --> 01:52:44.520
Well, that is overly generous,

2464
01:52:44.520 --> 01:52:48.640
I wanna thank you for really getting me to reflect

2465
01:52:48.640 --> 01:52:51.420
on a bunch of assumptions that I haven't questioned enough.

2466
01:52:51.420 --> 01:52:55.220
And I'm also just in addition to this being

2467
01:52:55.220 --> 01:52:57.210
an exhilarating conversation,

2468
01:52:57.210 --> 01:53:01.660
with a different kind of intensity than endurance sport,

2469
01:53:01.660 --> 01:53:03.950
I'm also just thrilled that I got to make a new friend.

2470
01:53:03.950 --> 01:53:06.400
Yeah cool, you and me both, I look forward

2471
01:53:06.400 --> 01:53:09.688
to meeting you in person at some time at some point.

2472
01:53:09.688 --> 01:53:11.050
And let's try to figure out

2473
01:53:11.050 --> 01:53:13.080
that endurance challenge thing, all right?

2474
01:53:13.080 --> 01:53:15.320
Thank you, and I wanna hear what your next challenges are

2475
01:53:15.320 --> 01:53:16.960
and figure it out if I can be helpful with them.

2476
